    year = "2009",
    publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
    doi = "10.1017/CBO9780511581434.002",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Kant proposes that nature can be read "as if" it is guiding humanity through unsocial sociability toward a cosmopolitan legal order and perpetual peace. This historical teleology is explicitly regulative rather than constitutive: we cannot know that nature pursues this purpose, but adopting this perspective as a heuristic allows us to detect rational threads in what otherwise appears as contingent chaos. History is a thought-experiment in applied teleology. RELEVANCE: Establishes the Kantian alternative to Hegelian constitutive teleology--the regulative/constitutive distinction is the central axis of the Kant/Hegel debate within the tradition and a key reference point for critics. The 2009 Cambridge edition (Rorty and Schmidt, eds.) provides the most comprehensive scholarly apparatus. POSITION: Regulative teleology grounded in practical reason; the idea of a universal history is a postulate for orientation in action, not a theoretical claim about historical mechanism.},
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Kant, primary-text, regulative-teleology, cosmopolitan, High",
    abstract = "This essay appears to have been occasioned by a passing remark made by Kant's colleague and follower Johann Schultz in a 1784 article in the Gotha Learned Papers. In order to make good on Schultz's remark, Kant wrote this article, which appeared in the Berlinische Monatsschrift late in the same year.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@book{pinkard2017history,
    author = "Pinkard, Terry",
    title = "Does History Make Sense? Hegel on the Historical Shapes of Justice",
    year = "2017",
    publisher = "Harvard University Press",
    doi = "10.4159/9780674978782",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Pinkard reconstructs Hegel's philosophy of history not as a teleological metaphysics of Spirit but as a theory of the historical shapes of justice--the normative orders through which freedom has been institutionalized and through which the demands of mutual recognition have been progressively articulated. History "makes sense" not because Spirit mechanically drives it forward but because agents navigate breakdowns in normative life, generating new shapes of ethical community through lived practice. RELEVANCE: Represents the most developed recent statement of the non-metaphysical reading of Hegel's philosophy of history and directly engages the tradition's self-understanding. The book establishes what critics who target Hegel's "teleology" or "Eurocentrism" are or should be engaging with. POSITION: Non-metaphysical, normative-practical reading of Hegel's historical teleology; history exhibits rational structure through the logic of institutional breakdown and reconstruction.},
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, secondary, non-metaphysical, justice, High",
    abstract = "Hegel’s philosophy of history—which most critics view as a theory of inevitable progress toward modern European civilization—is widely regarded as a failure today. Terry Pinkard’s spirited defense of the Hegelian view, based on a subtle understanding of human subjectivity, will play a central role in contemporary reevaluations of Hegel’s work.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@book{pippin1989hegelidealism,
    author = "Pippin, Robert B.",
    title = "Hegel's Idealism: The Satisfactions of Self-Consciousness",
    year = "1989",
    publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
    doi = "10.1017/cbo9780511621109",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Pippin argues that Hegel's idealism should be understood as completing Kant's transcendental project rather than reverting to pre-critical metaphysics. Hegel's "absolute idealism" is a radicalized Kantianism: where Kant left a gap between the transcendental conditions of experience and "things in themselves," Hegel shows that the very notion of a mind-independent "given" is incoherent. This de-individualizes and historicizes Kant's spontaneity, making rational self-determination into an inherently social and historical achievement. RELEVANCE: Pippin's non-metaphysical reading became the dominant Anglo-American interpretive framework and defines what many critics of Hegel are arguing against or with. Critics who claim Hegel's teleology is metaphysically unjustified must reckon with Pippin's argument that Hegel is not committed to strong metaphysical teleology in the traditional sense. POSITION: Non-metaphysical, neo-Kantian reading of Hegel; the Logic and Phenomenology complete rather than abandon Kantian critique.},
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, secondary, non-metaphysical, idealism, High",
    abstract = "This is the most important book on Hegel to have appeared in the past ten years. Robert Pippin offers a completely new interpretation of Hegel's idealism, which focuses on Hegel's appropriation and development of kant's theoretical project. Hegel is presented neither as a precritical metaphysician nor as a social theorist, but as a critical philosopher whose disagreements with Kant, especially on the issue of intuitions, enrich the idealist arguments against empiricism, realism and naturalism. In the face of the dismissal of absolute idealism as either unintelligible or implausible, Pippin explains and defends an original account of the philosophical basis for Hegel's claims about the historical and social nature of selfconsciousness, and so of knowledge itself.",
    abstract_source = "openalex",
    sep_context = {SEP Hegel entry: "Within English-speaking philosophy, the final quarter of the twentieth century saw something of a revival of serious interest in Hegel's philosophy with important works appearing such as those by H.S. Harris (1972, 1983, 1997), Charles Taylor (1975), Robert Pippin (1989) and Terry Pinkard (1994) in North America" (Life, Work, and Influence section). Also cited in the non-metaphysical/metaphysical debate section: "Robert Pippin has replied to early critics by elaborating on the senses in which the philosophical content of the post-Kantian Hegel might or might not be described as metaphysical, distancing his earlier interpretation from the label non-metaphysical (Pippin 1989, 2019)." (Revised Metaphysical Reaction section).}
}

@book{beiser2005hegel,
    author = "Beiser, Frederick",
    title = "Hegel",
    year = "2005",
    publisher = "Routledge",
    doi = "10.4324/9780203087053",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser defends a metaphysical reading of Hegel against the non-metaphysical interpretations of Pippin and Pinkard. For Beiser, Hegel's idealism involves substantive ontological commitments--the identity of thought and being, the objective rationality of the natural and social world--that cannot be deflated into a form of Kantianism. The philosophy of history is genuinely committed to Geist as a real, developing whole. RELEVANCE: Represents the strongest scholarly case for the traditional metaphysical reading that critics of speculative history typically have in mind when they target Hegel's teleology. The Pippin/Beiser debate defines the contested self-understanding of the tradition. POSITION: Metaphysical reading of Hegel; Geist is an objective, developing reality, not merely a practical or normative standpoint.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, secondary, metaphysical-reading, idealism, High",
    abstract = "Hegel (1770-1831) is one of the major philosophers of the nineteenth century. Many of the major philosophical movements of the twentieth century - from existentialism to analytic philosophy - grew out of reactions against Hegel. He is also one of the hardest philosophers to understand and his complex ideas, though rewarding, are often misunderstood. In this magisterial and lucid introduction, Frederick Beiser covers every major aspect of Hegel's thought. He places Hegel in the historical context of nineteenth-century Germany whilst clarifying the deep insights and originality of Hegel's philosophy.A masterpiece of clarity and scholarship, Hegel is both the ideal starting point for those coming to Hegel for the first time and essential reading for any student or scholar of nineteenth century philosophy.Additional features: glossary chapter summaries chronology annotated further reading.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@book{beiser2002germanidealism,
    author = "Beiser, Frederick C.",
    title = "German Idealism: The Struggle Against Subjectivism, 1781-1801",
    year = "2002",
    publisher = "Harvard University Press",
    doi = "10.4159/9780674020702",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser traces the development of German idealism from Kant through Fichte, Schelling, and early Hegel as a sustained attempt to overcome the subjective idealism implicit in Kant's framework. Each successive thinker sought to reconcile the subject-object division that Kant's Copernican Revolution seemed to leave in place, culminating in Schelling's philosophy of identity and Hegel's absolute idealism. This context shows that the speculative tradition in philosophy of history emerges from a coherent philosophical problematic about the place of reason in reality. RELEVANCE: Establishes the philosophical context within which Kant, Fichte, Schelling, and Hegel developed their views on history. For critics of the speculative tradition, understanding the movement as a whole is essential to avoid misrepresenting the tradition's aims. POSITION: Intellectual-historical account of the German idealist movement; establishes Schelling and Fichte as essential interlocutors alongside Kant and Hegel.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, German-idealism, Fichte, Schelling, secondary, High",
    abstract = {The current book by Adrian Johnston continues his extensive engagement with the work of Slavoj Žižek, and so with the question of a proper statement of a contemporary "dialectical materialism," an issue that for both of them hangs on a proper reading of Hegel's theoretical work. Johnston's bookŽižek's Ontology(2008) dealt with Žižek's work up untilThe Parallax View(2006). HisBadious, Žižek, and Political Transformations: The Cadence of Change(2009) deals with Žižek up untilIn Defense of Lost Causes(2008). The current volume deals predominantly with two works; a work Johnston often refers to as Žižek'schef d'ouevreor masterpiece,Less Than Nothing: Hegel and the Shadow of Dialectical Materialism(2012), and its subsequent restatement and reformulation,Absolute Recoil: Toward a New Foundation of Dialectical Materialism(2014). abstract\_source = {ndpr}, On the one hand, the topic of the current book is formulated in a way that would seem to be preaching to a small and select choir, whoever is worried about "Which comes first, the positivity of contingent material facticity or the negativity of a primordial Void. Schematically speaking, I defend the former option, while Žižek defends the latter." (p.xi) On the other hand, the engagement with Hegel raises a number of important philosophical issues much in the philosophical news, from the cogency of modern versions of naturalism and materialisms to the proper critique now needed of what is at this point a depressingly totalized, triumphant global capitalism. And there are several crucial issues of Hegel interpretation involved. However, the approach taken in the book limits it in other ways. It is relentlessly self-referential and self-promoting ("Žižek and I" is a constant refrain), often self-congratulatory, and frequently quite polemical, at times to the point of agitprop. "Anything short of this reckoning [Johnston's own position] signals a disrespectful underestimation throwing the doors wide open to a surreptitious replacement of Hegel with a dummy made for exploitation by post-Hegelian ventriloquists." (p.73) (Full disclosure: I am the main target of the chapter that concludes with this swipe. More to come below.) Much of the polemic sounds like an intra-party squabble about purity (as in, "who is thetruedialectical materialist"). Positions are "betrayed" (p.129); there are charges of unacceptable backsliding, slippage from the right position (p.147) and there is an unfortunate sectarian cast to the whole enterprise. (Žižek's work, by contrast, is refreshingly free of these characteristics.) Johnston seems to want mostly to call Žižek back to his better self, to offer an "internal critique" that states the Žižekian position more clearly than some of Žižek's own formulations. The main issue that unites Johnston and Žižek is the extraordinary claim that Hegel's Absolute Idealism is actually, when properly understood, "dialectical materialism," and, indeed, precisely the version modern critical theory now requires (that is, not the "historical materialism" of left-Hegelianism).[1]The materialism that results is not a reference to the putatively harmonious, fully rational, and in principle fully intelligible nature of Newtonian mechanics but a combination in some way of late psychoanalytic theory (the self-negation at the heart of Hegel is for Žižek a manifestation of Freud's death drive), Lacan, and the results of quantum physics and even string theory (or at least what are by now Sunday magazine summaries of such bodies of knowledge.) Their main disagreement (there are several minor ones) is what is expressed telegraphically in the passage quoted above. Johnston wants Hegel's philosophy of nature to have pride of place in dialectical materialism. He thinks Žižek focuses too much on theLogicand is too wedded to a Schellingian claim that the subject-object distinction (or the distinction between spontaneous subjectivity or freedom, and nature or natural determinism) can be neither a subjective nor objective distinction, and so must be some sort of "void," or "abyss," not even a "nothing" but "less than nothing." Or in other Hegelian terms, the substance-subject split is not a dualism per se; it is the self-diremption of substance, its "recoil" from itself. This is not a prioritization of substance because it is the gap, the split, the neither-one-nor-the-other that counts as the Hegelian Absolute. These are extraordinarily abstract terms and we stay pretty much at this level of abstraction throughout the book. After a short Preface reminding the reader of Žižek's understanding of theVerstand-Vernunft(understanding-reason) distinction (no abandonment of the former in moving to the latter but a working through of the former to reveal what it was unknowingly committed to all along), and an introductory first chapter, there are four more chapters and a long conclusion on drive and desire. The Introduction (1) discusses the problem of freedom and system in German philosophy. The chapters then discuss (2) the errors of the supposedly deflationist, subjective idealist, Kantian reading of Hegel that Johnston ascribes, quite inaccurately, to me; (3) the problem of contingency in Hegel's system; (4) What Johnston calls "materialism sans materialism" in Žižek; (5) German Idealism, biology and compatibilism, and then a Conclusion on the "(Meta)-dialectics of drive and desire." The first, introductory chapter will be familiar to anyone who knows the history of the growing dissatisfaction with what could be called reason's sufficiency unto itself, or the absoluteness and autonomy of pure apperceptive thought, in Kant and, so it was assumed, in Hegel, a moment of dissatisfaction already visible in Hölderlin's 1795 fragment "Urtheil und Seyn." The activity of thinking must, it was argued, have some "ground" in some sort of pre- or non-thought, what is prior to or a basis of thinking, out of which or in terms of which its activity can be accounted for. Empirically unaided, reflective pure thinking cannot be wholly self-positing as it is paradigmatically in Fichte, but also in Kant (the firstCritiqueis wholly reason's critique of itself) and in Hegel'sLogic(which for Johnston, should be understood to be equiprimordial with the Philosophies of Nature and Sprit, not that on which the latter essentially depend (p.75)). This of course is also part of the problem of understanding systematically the spontaneity necessary for freedom with the causal necessity of nature. If it is a result of thought's self-reflection that we are unavoidably committed to both positions, then we must have an ontology, an account of the real, in which both commitments could make sense. Johnston explains Žižek's dissatisfactions with Kant's two-world solution, and what he proposes as his own suggestion in the following formulation. It resonates with the summary above. Žižek, both inLess than Nothingand throughout his corpus, relentlessly pursues investigations regarding how being qua being must be thoroughly reconceptualized in light of the facts both that it sunders itself into the parallax split between subjectivity and objectivity and that subjective reflectivity or reflexivity continues to remain immanent, although nonetheless irreducible to it. (p.29) Or, what are gaps in our knowledge in Kant are "ontologized" by Žižek's reading of Hegel; they are gaps in being. This sort of thing is a frequent refrain in the book (see p.61, 144, inter alia; and "the gap (that separates us from the Thing) as the Real" (p.190))[2]and I was constantly puzzled by it. If being "sunders itself" just ends up meaning: we must deal with the fact that being includes subjects and objects, the world just comes this way, it has somehow resulted in this duality, then we are simply still faced with all our problems. (How could subjects know objects? How could subjects move objects, including a subject's body, around? How could objects be conscious? If these are illusory problems wrongly formulated, as I think Hegel believes, the sundering event does not help us understand why.) If "sunders itself" is supposed toexplainsomething, what accounts for the sundering and how does the sundering event help us understand this "immanence" but not "reducibility" (isn't that the old problem just restated?), and how would that help with these problems? Simply saying:nothingaccounts for it; it, the sundering event, is pure contingency (a frequent refrain too; it all arises out of the void), is certainly a conversation stopper, but it does not seem philosophically helpful. The same sort of thing is said of the "gap" between the factual and the normative. All is supposed to be solved when we realize that the gap "is immanent to the factual itself." (p.45) There are some positions which hold that the normative is "immanent" in the "factual"; Aristotle's, for example, if we want to put it that way. But the whole point there is: there is no gap. How does it do any philosophical work simply to assert that the gap is immanent to the factual? More serious problems emerge in Chapter Two. Johnston wants to criticize what he calls "deflationary" accounts of Hegel, which he characterizes as nonmetaphysical readings. He names a number of figures but the main culprit is yours truly. The chapter as a whole laboriously and repetitiously undertakes to show that Hegel was not Kant, was not a subjective but an absolute idealist, did not revert to trying to specify the categories necessary for the finite subject's experiencing the world, but had a metaphysical project, the goal of which was a robust account of being qua being. I am supposed to have replaced "absolute with subjective idealism wholesale." (p.59) None of this has anything whatsoever to do with the position I have attributed to Hegel for almost thirty years now. To restate the obvious: First, the only metaphysics Hegel wants to abandon is rationalist dogmatic metaphysics. (Johnston seems to understand this much.) No nonsensible noetic objects knowable by pure reason alone. No souls, immaterial minds, creator God, nores cogitans, no monads, Platonic Ideas, etc. Hegel frequently celebrates Kant's destruction of such an enterprise ("thing metaphysics," or the metaphysics of the "beyond").[3]Butof courseHegel has his own "metaphysics," and its closest analogue is Aristotle, not Leibniz or Spinoza or Plotinus. Besides, any moderately awake person reading Hegel on Kant from theDifferenzschriftonwards would not need to be reminded so tiresomely that Hegel, despite his frequently expressed profound admiration for Kant, completely rejects what he regards as Kant's subjective idealism. Johnston does not even mention Hegel's central concern, what he rightly understood to be the subjectivizing element in Kant's idealism, the pure subjective forms of intuition. And that is a telling point. Again, it is does not need to be pointed out to anyone that Hegel asserts "the identity of thought and being." But this has two primary dimensions. One concerns the "logical" determinations of anything at all, specified by pure thinking's dialectical self-determination in theLogic. But Hegel also wants to address directly Kant's concerns with the role of pure concepts in everything from perceptual experience to the interpretation of art works. He wants to insist that, while a conceptual and sensible element are distinguishable in perceptual experience, they are not separable. There is no two-step, or "imposition" application of concepts to an exogenous material. Perceptual experience is conceptual "all the way down." But the obvious fact that Hegel is concerned with correcting Kant on this issue should never be taken to mean that his "idealism"whollyconsists in a theory about the subjective conditions for the possibility of experience. This is just what Johnston has done, and it is a gross distortion. It is beyond me why Johnston thinks he needs to spend so many pages belaboring the stunningly obvious point that Hegel is not a subjective idealist. In theLogic, anyone interested in Hegel's theoretical philosophy must take on board several indisputable Hegelian principles, none of which I see present in Johnston's summaries.The Science of Logicis said to be "the science of pure thinking." Pure thinking is not (in theLogic, as opposed to thePhilosophy of Subjective Spirit) a mental event, or a subjective activity. It is pure thinking's inquiry into what is necessary for pure thinking to be possible, whatever or whoever is thinking. In just that sense, it is a logic. Pure thinking is understood as primarily judging, the truth bearer of all knowledge. Thinking is exclusively discursive, not intuitive, in no sense a perceptual power. It is pure activity; a productive power, and, as Hegel says over and over, it produces its own content. The object of pure thinking, he insists,is itself. Thinking, judging, is apperceptive; in any thinking or judging, such judging is reflective; judgingis, is not accompanied by, consciousness of judging. It is this feature that allows thinking's self-determination, in "producing" its "Denkbestimmungen," thought determinations or moments (Quantity, Quality, Relation and Modality, for example) to be simultaneously aware of what it has determined and whether it exhausts what is required for thinking to be a truth-bearer. With all this in play, Hegel insists unequivocally that only "now," after Kant, logicis metaphysics.[4]}
}

@incollection{beiser1993historicism,
    author = "Beiser, Frederick C.",
    editor = "Beiser, Frederick C.",
    title = "Hegel's Historicism",
    booktitle = "The Cambridge Companion to Hegel",
    year = "1993",
    publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
    pages = "270--300",
    doi = "10.1017/ccol0521382742.010",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser analyzes Hegel's historicism--the view that all standards of truth, value, and rationality are products of historical development and cannot be judged by transhistorical criteria--and distinguishes it from mere relativism. For Hegel, history has a rational structure that gives it normative authority; historicism and rationalism are reconciled through the claim that reason itself develops through history. Beiser examines the tension between Hegel's historicism and his absolutism (the claim that the system achieves absolute knowledge). RELEVANCE: Historicism is one of the defining and most contested theses of speculative philosophy of history. This article clarifies what Hegel meant by it, which is essential background for understanding both hermeneutic appropriations and analytic criticisms. POSITION: Defends Hegel's historicism as a coherent, non-relativist position that makes rationality itself historically constituted.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, historicism, secondary, High",
    abstract = {History cannot be consigned to a corner in Hegel's system, relegated to a few paragraphs near the end of the Encyclopedia or confined to his Lectures on the Philosophy of History For, as many scholars have long since recognized, history is central to Hegel's conception of philosophy. One of the most striking and characteristic features of Hegel's thought is that it historicizes philosophy, explaining its purpose, principles, and problems in historical terms. Rather than seeing philosophy as a timeless a priori reflection upon eternal forms, Hegel regards it as the self-consciousness of a specific culture, the articulation, defense, and criticism of its essential values and beliefs. This historical conception of philosophy is epitomized clearly by Hegel himself in the famous lines from the preface to his Philosophy of Right: "Philosophy is its own age comprehended in thought" (VII, 26).},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@book{avineri1972hegel,
    author = "Avineri, Shlomo",
    title = "Hegel's Theory of the Modern State",
    year = "1972",
    publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
    doi = "10.1017/cbo9781139171441",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Avineri reconstructs Hegel's political philosophy--including his philosophy of history--as a response to the political conditions of early nineteenth-century Europe, particularly the challenge of integrating individual freedom into modern state institutions. Avineri emphasizes that Hegel's state theory is neither conservative nor proto-totalitarian but reflects a sophisticated attempt to articulate the institutional conditions for freedom. The philosophy of history is integral to this: historical development is the progressive actualization of freedom in institutional form. RELEVANCE: A foundational work in the English-language reconstruction of Hegel's political and historical philosophy. Provides the institutional-political reading of historical teleology that many critics have engaged with, and counters both the right-Hegelian reading (Hegel as apologist for Prussian reaction) and Popper's charge that Hegel is a progenitor of totalitarianism. POSITION: Liberal-political reading of Hegel's historical teleology; freedom and rational institutions as the telos, not nationalism or state power.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, secondary, political-philosophy, freedom, High",
    abstract = "This study in English of Hegel's political philosophy presents an overall view of the development of Hegel's political thinking. The author has drawn on Hegel's philosophical works, his political tracts and his personal correspondence. Professor Avineri shows that although Hegel is primarily thought of as a philosopher of the state, he was much concerned with social problems and his concept of the state must be understood in this context.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@book{mccarney2000hegel,
    author = "Mccarney, Joseph",
    title = "Routledge Philosophy Guidebook to Hegel on History",
    year = "2000",
    publisher = "Routledge",
    doi = "10.4324/9780203136737",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: McCarney provides a systematic reconstruction of Hegel's philosophy of history through careful reading of the primary texts, arguing against both the dismissive tradition (Hegel as speculative fantasist) and overly apologetic reconstructions. He argues that Hegel's philosophy of history is a coherent, defensible account of historical rationality grounded in the development of freedom. McCarney engages extensively with the charges of Eurocentrism, racism, and political conservatism that have been leveled against Hegel. RELEVANCE: The most accessible and philosophically careful book-length guide to Hegel on history in English. Establishes the "target" for critical perspectives while also anticipating and engaging with key objections, making it valuable for mapping the debate. POSITION: Reconstructive and partially apologetic reading; argues for the philosophical defensibility of Hegel's historical scheme while acknowledging its limitations.},
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, secondary, guidebook, reconstruction, Medium",
    abstract = "Hegel's Introduction to the Philosophy of History remains one of the most profound and influential books on the philosophy of history. In clear and cogent terms this book:* examines the ideas and arguments of the Introduction to the Philosophy of History* explains key concepts of Hegel's system, a knowledge of which is essential for fully understanding his philosophy of history* assesses the continuing relevance of Hegel to the contemporary debate about the nature of history.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{kleingeld2008regulative,
    author = "Kleingeld, Pauline",
    title = "Kant on Historiography and the Use of Regulative Ideas",
    journal = "Studies in History and Philosophy of Science Part A",
    year = "2008",
    volume = "39",
    number = "4",
    pages = "523--528",
    doi = "10.1016/j.shpsa.2008.09.006",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld argues that Kant's philosophy of history is grounded in the regulative use of rational ideas as described in the Appendix to the Transcendental Dialectic in the Critique of Pure Reason. Kant's historiography is not a contingent political essay but is systematically tied to his account of reason's self-organization. Regulative ideas enable historians to approach history "as if" it exhibits purposive structure, without claiming constitutive knowledge of history's mechanism. RELEVANCE: Establishes the systematic place of Kant's philosophy of history within his critical architecture, directly bearing on the Kant/Hegel distinction (regulative vs. constitutive teleology) that is central to understanding the tradition. Shows that Kant's philosophy of history is philosophically integrated, not merely occasional. POSITION: Kantian regulative reading; connects the historical essays to the first Critique's Appendix to the Transcendental Dialectic.},
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Kant, regulative-teleology, historiography, secondary, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@article{kleingeld1999moral,
    author = "Kleingeld, Pauline",
    title = "Kant, History, and the Idea of Moral Development",
    journal = "History of Philosophy Quarterly",
    year = "1999",
    volume = "16",
    pages = "59--80",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld argues that Kant's historical teleology is primarily a theory of moral development: human history exhibits a progress not in metaphysical terms but in the gradual institutionalization of moral relations (republican constitutions, international law, cosmopolitan right). The telos of Kantian history is not happiness or prosperity but the conditions for moral self-legislation. This distinguishes Kant sharply from utilitarian or progressivist readings. RELEVANCE: Provides the most careful account of what progress means in Kant's philosophy of history, which is essential for evaluating criticisms of Kantian teleology as Eurocentric, paternalistic, or metaphysically inflated. Shows that Kant's moralized teleology differs importantly from Hegel's Geist-teleology. POSITION: Practical-moral reading of Kantian historical teleology; moral development and cosmopolitan right, not metaphysical self-realization of Spirit.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Kant, moral-development, cosmopolitan, teleology, High",
    abstract = "I examine the consistency of Kant's notion of moral progress as found in his philosophy of history. To many commentators, Kant's very idea of moral development has seemed inconsistent with basic tenets of his critical philosophy. This idea has seemed incompatible with his claims that the moral law is unconditionally and universally valid, that moral agency is noumenal and atemporal, and that all humans are equally free. Against these charges, I argue not only that Kant's notion of moral development is consistent, but also that the assumption of the possibility of moral progress is indispensible for Kant's moral theory",
    abstract_source = "core",
    sep_context = {SEP Progress entry cites Kleingeld 1999 in the context of Kant's regulative teleology: "While the idea that nature has a progressive plan sounds teleological, it is best read as a heuristic assumption or a regulative principle (Kleingeld 1999; Wood 1991; Moller 2021; Ypi 2010)." Cited in section 3 on Anthropology and Non-teleological Conceptions of Progress.}
}

@book{cambridge2009kanthistory,
    author = "Rorty, Amelie Oksenberg and Schmidt, James",
    title = "Kant's Idea for a Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Aim",
    year = "2009",
    publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
    doi = "10.1017/CBO9780511581434",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: This collected volume brings together leading scholars to analyze Kant's essay from multiple angles: its systematic place in the critical philosophy, its relation to Kant's moral and political theory, its historical context, and its reception. Contributions by Allison, Wood, Herman, Guyer, and others present competing reconstructions of Kantian historical teleology and its normative implications. RELEVANCE: The standard scholarly reference for Kant's philosophy of history. The volume explicitly addresses the question of whether Kant's teleology is regulative or constitutive, practical or theoretical, and connects the historical essays to the broader critical system. Essential for establishing what the Kantian variant of the speculative tradition actually claims. POSITION: Multi-perspective scholarly reconstruction; establishes the state of the art on Kantian historical teleology as of 2009.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Kant, teleology, universal-history, secondary, High",
    abstract = "Lively debates about narratives of historical progress, the conditions for international justice, and the implications of globalisation have prompted a renewed interest in Kant's Idea for a Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Aim. The essays in this volume, written by distinguished contributors, discuss the questions that are at the core of Kant's investigations. Does the study of history convey any philosophical insight? Can it provide political guidance? How are we to understand the destructive and bloody upheavals that constitute so much of human experience? What connections, if any, can be traced between politics, economics, and morality? What is the relation between the rule of law in the nation state and the advancement of a cosmopolitan political order? These questions and others are examined and discussed in a book that will be of interest to philosophers, social and political theorists, and intellectual and cultural historians.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@incollection{allison2009teleology,
    author = "Allison, Henry E.",
    editor = "Rorty, Amelie Oksenberg and Schmidt, James",
    title = "Teleology and History in Kant: The Critical Foundations of Kant's Philosophy of History",
    booktitle = "Kant's Idea for a Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Aim",
    year = "2009",
    publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
    doi = "10.1017/cbo9780511581434.003",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Allison examines the philosophical foundations of Kant's philosophy of history in the critical system, arguing that the teleological framework of the historical essays is grounded in the reflective judgment of the third Critique rather than in constitutive reason. Historical teleology for Kant is a regulative principle of reflective judgment applied to human affairs--it enables us to see human history as purposive without committing us to mechanical or theological determinism. RELEVANCE: Provides a rigorous account of the systematic relationship between Kant's third Critique and his philosophy of history, directly bearing on the key distinction between Kantian and Hegelian teleology. Allison's analysis helps determine precisely what kind of "teleology" Kant is committed to and what criticisms apply to it. POSITION: Third-Critique grounding of Kantian historical teleology; regulative principle of reflective judgment, not constitutive reason.},
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Kant, teleology, reflective-judgment, secondary, High",
    abstract = {Although the title of Kant's essay Idea for Universal History with a Cosmopolitan Aim indicates its central theme, it reveals little or nothing about its underlying methodology and its connection with the emerging critical philosophy. Indeed, as far as the title is concerned, the only hint of a connection with the latter is provided by the inclusion of the term "Idea." This is a technical term for Kant referring to concepts of reason, which, as distinct from concepts of the understanding, whose legitimate use is restricted to possible experience, involve the thought of an absolute totality or completeness that can never be met with in a possible experience and is, therefore, "transcendent" with respect to the latter. In the first Critique, Kant's appealed to the Platonic republic and a constitution that provides for "the greatest human freedom according to laws that permit the freedom of each to exist together with that of others" as examples of such ideas (A 316/B 372–3); but his focus was on the "transcendental Ideas" (the soul, the world, and God), which arise from extending certain concepts of the understanding to the "unconditioned," thereby producing the thought of a complete systematic unity. While illusory, in the sense that no real object corresponds to them, these Ideas nonetheless play an essential regulative role in guiding the understanding in its endemic search for unity in experience.},
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@book{beiser1993cambridge,
    author = "Beiser, Frederick C.",
    title = "The Cambridge Companion to Hegel",
    year = "1993",
    publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
    doi = "10.1017/ccol0521382742",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: This handbook provides systematic coverage of Hegel's major works and themes through essays by leading scholars, including contributions on Hegel's logic, phenomenology, philosophy of right, philosophy of history, philosophy of religion, and aesthetics. The volume situates Hegel in his historical context while engaging with contemporary debates about metaphysics, historicism, and the relevance of the Hegelian system. RELEVANCE: Foundational reference volume for understanding the scholarly landscape around Hegel. The entries on Hegel's historicism, dialectics, and philosophy of history directly serve as the baseline against which critics of the speculative tradition must position themselves. POSITION: Introductory-reference volume covering all aspects of Hegel's system; pivotal for the Anglo-American scholarly reception of Hegel from the 1990s onward.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, secondary, reference, handbook, Medium",
    abstract = "Few thinkers are more controversial in the history of philosophy than Hegel. He has been dismissed as a charlatan and obscurantist, but also praised as one of the greatest thinkers in modern philosophy. No one interested in philosophy can afford to ignore him. This volume considers all the major aspects of Hegel's work: epistemology, logic, ethics, political philosophy, aesthetics, philosophy of history, philosophy of religion. Special attention is devoted to problems in the interpretation of Hegel: the unity of the Phenomenology of Spirit; the value of the dialectical method; the status of his logic; the nature of his politics. A final group of chapters treats Hegel's complex historical legacy: the development of Hegelianism and its growth into a left and right-wing school; the relation of Hegel and Marx; and the subtle connections between Hegel and contemporary analytic philosophy.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@book{pinkard2002german,
    author = "Pinkard, Terry",
    title = "German Philosophy 1760--1860: The Legacy of Idealism",
    year = "2002",
    publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
    doi = "10.1017/cbo9780511801846",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Pinkard provides an intellectual history of German philosophy from Kant through Hegel, Schopenhauer, Feuerbach, and Marx, showing how each thinker responded to problems bequeathed by Kant's critical revolution. The chapter on Hegel traces the development of his philosophy of history as a response to the fragmentation of modern life that Kant's dualisms exposed. Fichte and Schelling are treated as essential transitional figures. RELEVANCE: Situates the speculative tradition in philosophy of history within its intellectual-historical context, showing how Kant, Fichte, Schelling, and Hegel form a continuous problematic rather than isolated positions. Essential background for understanding why Hegel's philosophy of history takes the shape it does. POSITION: Intellectual-historical contextualization; the speculative tradition emerges from a sustained engagement with problems in Kant's critical philosophy.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, German-idealism, secondary, intellectual-history, Medium",
    abstract = "In the second half of the eighteenth century, German philosophy came for a while to dominate European philosophy. It changed the way in which not only Europeans, but people all over the world, conceived of themselves and thought about nature, religion, human history, politics, and the structure of the human mind. In this rich and wide-ranging book, Terry Pinkard interweaves the story of 'Germany' - changing during this period from a loose collection of principalities into a newly-emerged nation with a distinctive culture - with an examination of the currents and complexities of its developing philosophical thought. He examines the dominant influence of Kant, with his revolutionary emphasis on 'self-determination', and traces this influence through the development of romanticism and idealism to the critiques of post-Kantian thinkers such as Schopenhauer and Kierkegaard. His book will interest a range of readers in the history of philosophy, cultural history and the history of ideas.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{bernasconi2000racial,
  author = {Bernasconi, Robert},
  title = {With What Must the Philosophy of World History Begin? On the Racial Basis of {Hegel}'s Eurocentrism},
  journal = {Nineteenth-Century Contexts},
  year = {2000},
  volume = {22},
  number = {2},
  pages = {171--201},
  doi = {10.1080/08905490008583507},
  abstract = {(2000). With what must the philosophy of world history begin? On the racial basis of Hegel's eurocentrism. Nineteenth-Century Contexts: Vol. 22, Race and social theory, pp. 171-201.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Bernasconi argues that Hegel's Eurocentrism is not a
  contingent historical prejudice but is grounded in the racial hierarchy
  built into the philosophy of subjective spirit. The categorization of
  Africans as outside history in the Lectures on the Philosophy of World
  History follows systematically from the racial taxonomy in the
  Encyclopaedia's treatment of the natural soul, where Hegel assigns
  different degrees of spiritual capacity to different races. This is not
  incidental commentary but is architecturally connected to Hegel's account
  of how Geist becomes objective in world history.

  RELEVANCE: The foundational philosophical argument that Hegel's racial
  categorization is not separable from the speculative architecture. Establishes
  the claim that any rehabilitation of Hegelian philosophy of history must
  confront the racial structure, not merely excise peripheral comments. Cross-
  domain connection to Domain 1 (Hegel's philosophy of history) where
  Bernasconi's work is a major interpretive reference.

  POSITION: Racial structure thesis -- Hegel's Eurocentrism is constitutively
  racial, not incidentally so.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Bernasconi, Hegel, race, Eurocentrism, philosophy-of-history, High, domain-1-crossref}
}

@article{bernasconi2024egypt,
    author = "Bernasconi, Robert",
    title = "Hegel and Egypt's African Element",
    journal = "Hegel Bulletin",
    year = "2024",
    volume = "45",
    pages = "6--22",
    doi = "10.1017/hgl.2024.2",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Bernasconi examines Hegel's treatment of ancient Egypt in the Lectures on the Philosophy of World History, arguing that Hegel systematically misrepresents Egypt's African character in order to preserve a historical scheme in which Africa has no role. Egypt is positioned as a transition between Africa (excluded from world-history) and Greece (the origin of Western civilization), and this positioning requires suppressing Egypt's African connections. RELEVANCE: Updates and deepens the race-critical challenge to Hegel's historical scheme. As recent scholarship, it represents the current state of postcolonial critique of the speculative tradition, directly engaging with how Hegel's teleological structure necessitates particular historical distortions. POSITION: Extends and refines the postcolonial/race-critical reading of Hegel's philosophy of history; demonstrates ongoing relevance of Bernasconi's earlier critique.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, race, Africa, postcolonial, critical, Medium",
    abstract = "Abstract Contrary to the widespread view that Hegel excluded Africa from what he called world history proper, the specifically African element of Egypt was indispensable to his account of the pivotal dialectical moment that saw spirit's release from its immersion in nature. Hegel's racist caricature of Africans in the early part of the lectures was not gratuitous, something that commentators can leave to one side. It was integral to his dialectical account of world history because it served to generate the contradiction that saw the Persian Empire give way to Greece. This understanding is confirmed by the newly completed four-volume edition of Hegel's Lectures on the Philosophy of World History in the Gesammelte Werke. These new volumes also enable us to gain critical insight into how the editors of the first two published versions of the lectures, Eduard Gans and Karl Hegel, sought in their different ways to shape the reception of Hegel's lectures.",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@article{ankersmit2021thorn,
    author = "Ankersmit, Frank",
    title = "The Thorn of History: Unintended Consequences and Speculative Philosophy of History",
    journal = "History and Theory",
    year = "2021",
    doi = "10.1111/hith.12202",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Ankersmit argues that Hegel's "cunning of reason"--the idea that unintended consequences serve the purposes of World Spirit--contains an important philosophical insight that can be detached from Hegel's metaphysics. Unintended consequences are the genuine stuff of history, and a non-metaphysical speculative philosophy of history can be built from this insight. Ankersmit connects this to Anthropocene thinking, suggesting that climate change represents the ultimate unintended consequence. RELEVANCE: Represents a contemporary attempt to rehabilitate speculative philosophy of history after the collapse of Hegelian metaphysics. Shows that the tradition's insights need not depend on its most contested commitments, while also illustrating how the cunning-of-reason thesis has been understood and transformed. POSITION: Neo-speculative reading; defends a reconstituted speculative philosophy of history grounded in the logic of unintended consequences, without metaphysical commitment to Geist.},
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, speculative-history, cunning-of-reason, Hegel, contemporary, Medium",
    abstract = "Most, if not all, readers of this journal will be familiar with Hegel’s notion of “the Cunning of Reason,” which refers to the idea that the unintended consequences of human action should bring about the self-realization of the Mind or the Spirit. What Hegel left to his reader’s imagination was the question of how order could arise from the chaos of unintended consequences. Nevertheless, Hegel’s notion of the unintended consequences of human action is a most fruitful one. In this article, I argue that unintended consequences are “the stuff” from which history is made and that a speculative philosophy of history can be built from them too. This argument infers a speculative philosophy of history from historical writing and not a variant of historical writing from a speculative philosophy of history, as is normally the case. Finally, I discuss how global warming and the impending climate catastrophe can be said to be the prototypical unintended consequences of human action, or to speak with Saddam Hussein, “the mother of all” unintended consequences. This article ends, therefore, with a discussion of how the notion of unintended consequences may contribute to a better understanding of what the Anthropocene must mean for our historical consciousness and for how we locate ourselves in the evolution of history since the dawn of humanity down to the present, in which dark clouds gather over our collective future. is of shift, the of in of as vision—in domains—is",
    abstract_source = "s2"
}

@book{bourke2023hegelrevolutions,
    author = "Bourke, Richard",
    title = "Hegel's World Revolutions",
    year = "2023",
    publisher = "Princeton University Press",
    doi = "10.1353/book.115384",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Bourke provides a historical and philosophical account of Hegel's engagement with the major political revolutions of his era--the French Revolution, the Napoleonic transformations, and the conservative reaction--situating the philosophy of history within Hegel's lived political context. The book argues that understanding Hegel's historical claims requires attending to the concrete political circumstances that shaped his thinking about freedom, statehood, and historical development. RELEVANCE: Recent major work that contextualizes Hegel's philosophy of history historically, providing a counterpoint to purely systematic reconstructions. Relevant for understanding the conditions under which Hegel developed his speculative scheme and for evaluating criticisms that the scheme is ideologically motivated. POSITION: Historical-contextualist reading of Hegel's philosophy of history; political events as shaping and testing Hegel's systematic claims.",
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, political-history, contextual, recent, Medium",
    abstract = "Paul Ricoeur famously singled out Marx, Nietzsche, and Freud as three “masters of suspicion”, bent on looking beneath the surface of modern Western reason (1970: 32). Despite their many differences, all three denounced Enlightenment rationalism as an artificial construction, rooted in a covert set of economic or psychological motivations, and their criticisms have cast a long historical shadow. InHegel’s World Revolutions, Richard Bourke aims to challenge this critical trend. He focuses, more precisely, on how the cultural pessimism that emerged during the late nineteenth century has shaped contemporary thought and on the favour it currently enjoys in academic and intellectual circles. In his eyes, “what started out as an eccentric tradition of historical reflection”, launched by Nietzsche and generalized by Heidegger, Adorno and Foucault, “now occupies a central place on university courses”. With the advance of modern and postmodern critical theory, “whole generations have been coaxed into a posture of suspicion” (ix) and led to mistrust the rational conquests of the modern world. abstract\_source = {ndpr}, It is questionable whether this attitude is as influential as Bourke suggests, given the current academic dominance of Anglo-American liberal theory. Still, it is certainly true that the Enlightenment’s universalist values are now dismissed, in some quarters, as thoroughly biased, coercive or Eurocentric, and that the notion of historical progress is rejected by many as hopelessly ideological. Opposing this general trend, Bourke seeks to reaffirm the West’s rationalist legacy. And he does so by turning to Hegel’s philosophy of history and its unique emphasis on modernity’s emancipatory potential. Hegel, of course, is no wide-eyed optimist, but a sophisticated critic of the Enlightenment. Instead of negating its achievements, however, he highlights their unilateral nature and locates them within the wider positive process whereby reason attains reality. On his view, the ideals of freedom and equality characteristic of Western modernity are irreducible to their flawed historical actualization. Although the modern world “is still replete with glaring cases of injustice. . .its history records a process of liberation” (ix–x) that must not be downplayed or relativized. This approach is not without precedent. A similar emphasis on the human capacity for self-improvement is already found in Kant, who also proposes a philosophical reconstruction of history aimed at bringing out the “regular course of improvement” leading from the ancient Greek polis to the modern constitutional state (1996: 119). Moreover, Kant also measures the rationality of a given society by the extent to which it promotes human freedom. However, his philosophy of history is still informed by amoralunderstanding of political progress: he conceives of political agents as constitutively torn between selflessness and self-interest, and views every civilizational breakthrough as a victory of the former over the latter. For Hegel, political change is not confined to this dual framework. It hinges on a complex historical interplay between human intentions and the actual norms and institutions in which they are grounded. While Kant remains focused on the fulfilment of a timeless rational ideal, Hegel treats reason as an inherently dynamic goal, whose nature can only be fully grasped in the process of its actualization. Hegel’s account of world history is structured around a series of epochal shifts: the transition from the pre-classical world to Greek civilization, from its decline to the rise of the Roman empire, and from the latter’s decline to the advent of the modern “Germanic” realm. Bourke condenses this long historical movement into two main transformative axes, discussed in the book’s first and second parts. He starts by charting humankind’s moral evolution, which for Hegel begins with the dawn of the Christian world. While Judaism emphasized human impotence in the face of an all-mighty God, Christianity insisted on the moral freedom to distinguish right from wrong. Yet this new doctrine was at odds with mundane existence, prompting the faithful to either flee into a spiritual “void” or enmesh themselves in worldly politics. Later, this division would lead to Luther’s Reformation, and later still to the Kantian distinction between duty and inclination. Rejecting these dualisms, Hegel called for a reconciliation of the autonomous and the heteronomous dimensions of morality. Bourke reconstructs his search for this reconciliation during the 1790s with admirable skill. His discussion of Hegel’s early writings, notorious for their thematic dispersion, is clear and sophisticated. The book’s second part is also about this attempt at reconciliation, but applied more directly to the political realm. Extending the scope of his analysis to Hegel’s mature writings, Bourke retraces his views on the evolution of political freedom in medieval and modern Europe. Central to this account is Hegel’s complex assessment of the French Revolution. Although he saw the upheaval in France as a watershed moment, he did not treat it as an isolated event, but as part of a wider historical trend, fueled by the decline of feudal nobility and culminating in Jacobinism and its demise. Hegel’s criticisms of the revolutionary process are emphasized in order to challenge his reputation as a champion of the French Revolution. The topic is still controversial among Hegel scholars, and a classical bone of contention between more conservative and more progressive readings of his work, but Bourke convincingly shows that Hegel associated the revolution with yet another “flight from ‘actuality’” (169), at odds with its concrete ethical context. Finally, the book’s third part deals with the modern reception of Hegel’s ideas. After a steady decline in the second half of the 19th century, his work was rehabilitated in Germany by Wilhem Dilthey, Friedrich Meinecke, and Franz Rosenzweig, and later also abroad by Georg Lukács, Jean Wahl, and Alexandre Kojève. However, the horrors of two world wars led many to reject Hegel’s belief in historical progress. Cultural advances were regarded as mere “fragments of a larger pattern of deterioration” (250) which denied history any redeeming value. On the one hand, critics like Heidegger, Horkheimer or Adorno grounded their pessimistic accounts of modernity in a global indictment of “occidental patterns of thought” (226); on the other, Strauss and Arendt sought to overcome modernity’s failures by resuscitating ancient conceptions of politics and society. From a Hegelian perspective, both strategies are misguided: past worldviews are not to be simply endorsed or rejected, but recognized as different stages in the historical actualization of reason. Hegel’s World Revolutionssuccessfully combines solid philosophical argumentation with careful historical analysis. Retracing Hegel’s intellectual path from his early writings to his late Berlin lectures, the book offers illuminating insights into the formation of his political thought. Unfortunately, this chronological approach leaves little room for a systematic discussion of thornier hermeneutical issues, such as the exact relationship between philosophy and historical reality. Bourke is somewhat ambiguous in this regard: while he concedes that philosophy can be “a determinant of historical change”, if only in certain periods and to a variable degree (151), he sometimes appears to reduce the philosopher to a sophisticated chronicler of bygone eras. Hegel, he claims, “denied that thinkers could offer practical direction” (215), settling instead for a critical rearticulation of the past. Yet what one finds in many key texts is arguably a more complex dialectical picture, in which philosophy is both a reconstruction of the past and, at the same time, a theoretical anticipation of a new historical era. Born out of the “breakdown” of a given cultural paradigm, philosophy is “the inner birthplace of the spirit that emerges later as actuality” (Hegel 2009: 67). A second topic left unexplored is the actual merit of Hegel’s theory of the state. And here two further issues are worth mentioning. First, his resistance to popular democracy is justified by a commitment to avoiding political changes “bound to prove counterproductive under conditions as they existed” (119). For Axel Honneth, likewise, Hegel was against making “unrealistic demands on social behaviour” (2014: 8). However, a defence of his position along these lines implies a previous determination of what a given age is and is not ready to accept. And Hegel himself recognizes that support for popular sovereignty had become common by the early nineteenth century (2008: §301R), and was therefore already within the horizon of his age. Second, Bourke insists that we are not Hegel’s contemporaries, thereby suggesting not only that his political views cannot be directly revived, but also that we should refrain from passing judgement on them. And the latter principle is strictly observed throughoutHegel’s World Revolutions, where even his most contentious positions are sympathetically presented. Yet Hegel was also criticized for being inconsistent with his own philosophical principles. A case in point is the obvious tension between his affirmation of freedom and self-determination and his approval of hereditary monarchy, limited political participation, and aristocratic birth rights. In my view, to highlight these inconsistencies is not to betray the “spirit” of his philosophy, but to clarify the extent to which it remains relevant."
}

@misc{raysmith2025hegel,
    author = "Raysmith, Thomas",
    title = "Hegel and the Problem of the History of Philosophy",
    year = "2025",
    doi = "10.5040/9781350423794",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Raysmith examines Hegel's justification for the claim that philosophy has a history, reconstructing what he calls the "structure of exemplarity"--the dynamic reciprocity between universality, particularity, and singularity--as the logical structure enabling the history of philosophy. The book provides a historical reconstruction from Kant through Fichte and Schelling to Hegel, showing how Hegel's specific response to Kant's ahistorical conception of philosophy generates the philosophical architecture of his Lectures on the History of Philosophy. RELEVANCE: Addresses the logical-philosophical foundations of Hegel's speculative use of history, connecting the philosophy of history to the Logic and showing how Hegel's historical claims flow from his systematic commitments. Directly bears on the question of whether Hegel's speculative teleology is philosophically coherent. POSITION: Systematic-logical account of Hegel's philosophy of history; the history of philosophy as structured by the Logic's categories of universality, particularity, and singularity.},
    keywords = "speculative-tradition-foundations, Hegel, logic, history-of-philosophy, recent, Low, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Drawing on the work of major philosophers in 18th and 19th-century German idealism, Thomas Raysmith critically examines G. W. F. Hegel's justification for the claim that philosophy has a history. abstract\_source = {s2}, While Kant regarded philosophy as ahistorical, Hegel considered it to be a discipline that is necessarily historical, and elaborated a 'logical structure' that was supposed to allow it to have a history. Calling this structure, which Hegel took to be the fundamental structure of thought itself, 'the structure of exemplarity', Raysmith presents it as a dynamic reciprocity between universality, particularity and singularity. He provides a historical reconstruction of the shifting conceptions of philosophy from Kant, through J. G. Fichte and F. W. J. Schelling, to Hegel, and offers a systematic analysis of Hegel'sScience of Logicbased on a close, critical reading. Offering a compelling and novel reading of Hegel's thought,Hegel and the Problem of the History of Philosophyis a groundbreaking work for students and scholars of German idealism and the history of philosophy more broadly."
}

@article{hempel1942function,
    author = "Hempel, Carl G.",
    title = "The Function of General Laws in History",
    journal = "The Journal of Philosophy",
    year = "1942",
    volume = "39",
    pages = "35--48",
    doi = "10.2307/2017635",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Hempel argues that genuinely explanatory historical accounts must conform to the deductive-nomological (covering-law) model: an explanandum is explained when it follows from a set of general laws together with antecedent conditions. Where historians cannot state explicit laws, they offer "explanation sketches" -- incomplete surrogates that point toward but fall short of scientific explanation. The paper explicitly treats history as continuous with natural science in its explanatory logic. RELEVANCE: This is the founding document of the covering-law controversy in analytic philosophy of history. It provides the positivist standard against which Dray, Walsh, and Mink all react, and it directly implies that teleological explanations characteristic of speculative philosophy of history (Hegel, Kant) fail as explanations because they substitute purposive narrative for subsumption under empirical law. POSITION: Logical-empiricist (positivist) account of historical explanation; foundational statement of the analytic critique that scientific history leaves no room for speculative teleology.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, covering-law, historical-explanation, positivism, High",
    abstract = "1. It is a rather widely held opinion that history, in contradistinction to the so-called physical sciences, is concerned with the description of particular events of the past rather than with the search for general laws which might govern those events. As a characterization of the type of problem in which some historians are mainly interested, this view probably can not be denied; as a statement of the theoretical function of general laws in scientific historical research, it is certainly unacceptable. The following considerations are an attempt to substantiate this point by showing in some detail that general laws have quite analogous functions in history and in the natural sciences, that they form an indispensable instrument of historical research, and that they even constitute the common basis of various procedures which are often considered as characteristic of the social in contradistinction to the natural sciences.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{hempel1948studies,
    author = "Hempel, Carl G. and Oppenheim, Paul",
    title = "Studies in the Logic of Explanation",
    journal = "Philosophy of Science",
    year = "1948",
    volume = "15",
    pages = "135--175",
    doi = "10.1086/286983",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Hempel and Oppenheim give the full formal statement of the deductive-nomological model of scientific explanation, defining explanans and explanandum, the requirements of nomological sufficiency, and the distinction between explanation and prediction. This formalization became the standard against which all alternatives -- including historical explanation -- were measured. RELEVANCE: The canonical statement of D-N explanation underpins Hempel's 1942 claim about history: since historical accounts generally lack explicit universal laws, they fail the D-N standard. Any project on speculative philosophy of history must engage this model, because speculative teleology (Hegel's Geist, Kant's purposiveness) produces prediction-like claims about history's endpoint that fail D-N requirements while pretending to explanation. POSITION: Core logical empiricist model of scientific explanation; sets the standard that makes speculative historical narrative scientifically illegitimate.",
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, covering-law, scientific-explanation, logical-empiricism, High",
    abstract = "To explain the phenomena in the world of our experience, to answer the question “why?” rather than only the question “what?”, is one of the foremost objectives of all rational inquiry; and especially, scientific research in its various branches strives to go beyond a mere description of its subject matter by providing an explanation of the phenomena it investigates. While there is rather general agreement about this chief objective of science, there exists considerable difference of opinion as to the function and the essential characteristics of scientific explanation. In the present essay, an attempt will be made to shed some light on these issues by means of an elementary survey of the basic pattern of scientific explanation and a subsequent more rigorous analysis of the concept of law and of the logical structure of explanatory arguments.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{mandelbaum1961covering,
    author = "Mandelbaum, Maurice",
    title = "Historical Explanation: The Problem of `Covering Laws'",
    journal = "History and Theory",
    year = "1961",
    volume = "1",
    doi = "10.2307/2504314",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Mandelbaum critically examines Hempel's covering-law model and argues that typical historical explanations do not and need not subsume events under general laws. He distinguishes between genetic explanations (tracing a sequence of causally connected events) and nomological explanations, arguing the former are genuinely explanatory on their own terms. This resists Hempel's assimilation of history to natural science without abandoning the goal of objective historical knowledge. RELEVANCE: Mandelbaum's critique opens space between the covering-law model and speculative narrative by defending a form of causal-genetic explanation that is neither nomological nor teleological. This is relevant to the project's account of how analytic philosophers carved out a "critical" middle ground rejecting both Hempel's scientism and Hegel's teleology. POSITION: Anti-positivist analytic critique of covering-law model; defends genetic explanation as irreducible to nomological subsumption.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, covering-law, historical-explanation, genetic-explanation, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: pages",
    abstract = "In recent years the question of what constitutes an historical explanation has probably been more frequently discussed by English and American philosophers than has any other question concerning history. Willam Dray's interesting and influential book, Laws and Explanation in History, is perhaps the focal point for this discussion, and from that book I shall borrow the term using it (as does Dray) to refer to that group of theorists which includes Popper, Hempel, and Gardiner, among others. But since I shall need some term to denote those who have recently reacted against the views of these theorists, as have Professors Dray, Donagan, Nowell-Smith and Berlin, I shall (if I may) use the term to refer to them. I do so not in order to suggest that their works are merely reactions against the views of the covering-law theorists, but rather in order to distinguish them from another group of philosophers of history who also reject the covering law model of explanation, namely, the idealists.1 To be sure, there are some points at which Professors Dray, Donagan, and NowellSmith seem to make common cause with idealists such as Croce or Collingwood or Oakeshott; and the example of W. H. Walsh shows how closely the two positions may seem to approach one another. However, what characterizes the starting point of the reactionists is their assumption that a proper analysis of historical explanation must conform to the statements which historians actually make when they are giving what they take to be explanations of particular occurrences. The idealists assuredly made no such assumption; nor would idealist accounts of what constitutes an historical explanation actually fit this criterion of adequacy. Furthermore, the reactionists do not accept the general arguments by means of which idealists have attacked non-idealist theories, and in their frequent discussions of Collingwood they have been apt to expunge or radically reinterpret his more general meta-",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{watkins1952ideal,
    author = "Watkins, J. W. N.",
    title = "Ideal Types and Historical Explanation",
    journal = "The British Journal for the Philosophy of Science",
    year = "1952",
    volume = "3",
    pages = "22--43",
    doi = "10.1093/bjps/iii.9.22",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Watkins defends "methodological individualism" as the appropriate framework for historical explanation: all social phenomena must ultimately be explained through the dispositions, beliefs, and actions of individuals. He argues that Weberian ideal types are heuristic devices for generating such explanations, not Platonic essences or structural laws. This implicitly targets holistic and teleological accounts (Hegelian Volksgeist, Marxist modes of production) as explanatorily vacuous. RELEVANCE: Methodological individualism became a core analytic commitment setting analytic philosophy of history against speculative holism. The argument that social wholes (classes, nations, historical epochs) are not explanatory entities directly challenges the Hegelian picture in which Spirit or Reason explains historical events by reference to transpersonal teleological forces. POSITION: Methodological-individualist analytic critique of holistic explanation; aligned with Popper's anti-historicism.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, methodological-individualism, ideal-types, historical-explanation, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract"
}

@book{danto1965analytical,
    author = "Danto, Arthur C.",
    title = "Analytical Philosophy of History",
    year = "1965",
    publisher = "Cambridge University Press",
    address = "Cambridge",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Danto provides the most systematic early analytic treatment of both epistemological and ontological questions in philosophy of history. His central contribution is the concept of "narrative sentences" -- sentences describing an earlier event in terms of a later one unknown to contemporaries (e.g., "The Thirty Years War began in 1618"). Since narrative sentences presuppose the completion of processes not yet finished, a perfect "Ideal Chronicler" narrating the present in real time could never write them: narrative knowledge is essentially retrospective. This structural argument dismantles speculative philosophy of history by showing that "narrating the future" -- the ambition of Hegel, Toynbee, or Spengler -- involves not prophecy but logical confusion about the tense-structure of historical description. RELEVANCE: Danto's argument from narrative sentences is the most formally rigorous analytic argument that speculative philosophy of history is not merely empirically unverifiable but logically incoherent: it confuses the retrospective standpoint constitutive of historical narrative with prospective knowledge. This is central to any project on analytic criticisms of Hegel and Kant. POSITION: Analytic-linguistic critique of substantive philosophy of history via narrative-sentence analysis; establishes the impossibility of narrating the present historically, thus refuting speculative teleology.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, narrative-sentences, substantive-history, danto, High",
    abstract = "Артур Данто полагает, что задача философии не в том, чтобы размышлять или говорить о мире, а, скорее, в том, чтобы анализировать способы размышлений и высказываний о мире. Его книга — первый на русском языке анализ исторического мышления и языка, открывающий структуру самой истории",
    abstract_source = "core"
}

@article{danto1956explanations,
    author = "Danto, Arthur C.",
    title = "On Explanations in History",
    journal = "Philosophy of Science",
    year = "1956",
    volume = "23",
    pages = "15--30",
    doi = "10.1086/287457",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: An early paper in which Danto examines the structure of historical explanations and their relation to general law explanations, anticipating the narrative-sentence framework of his 1965 book. Danto argues that historical explanation has a distinctive form tied to the narration of sequences, not reducible to subsumption under covering laws while also not merely anecdotal. RELEVANCE: This precursor to Analytical Philosophy of History shows Danto working out the position that historical knowledge is structurally distinct from both natural-scientific explanation and speculative narration, helping trace the development of the analytic/speculative distinction. POSITION: Early development of Danto's distinctive analytic account of historical explanation distinct from both Hempelian and speculative models.",
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, historical-explanation, danto, Medium",
    abstract = "Whether or not history is, or could be, or ought to be a science, depends in part upon how the words “science” and “history” are to be used. But if one of the criteria of a science is an ability to provide explanations of large numbers of events by means of a small number of general laws, it then becomes in part a question of whether or not history does, or can, provide explanations of this sort for the phenomena which concern it. It is common knowledge that history, at the present time, displays no obvious instance of such an explanation; and I shall try to argue that, though the phenomena with which history deals could conceivably be explained in a “scientific” manner, it is not the office of history to provide such explanations, nor does its lack of explanations in any way diminish either the effectiveness or importance of history. So if my conclusions here suggest that history is not a science, and could not be one, they also suggest that it ought not to be one, but that it is a special and irreducible activity of the human spirit, with a function and justification of its own.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{dray1957laws,
    author = "Dray, William H.",
    title = "Laws and Explanation in History",
    year = "1957",
    address = "Oxford",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Dray mounts a sustained critique of the covering-law model as applied to history. His main positive alternative is the "rational explanation" model: to explain a historical agent's action is to show that it was the "appropriate" or "reasonable" response to their situation given their beliefs and goals -- a form of explanation that makes no use of general empirical laws but appeals to standards of practical rationality. Dray also develops a detailed account of re-enactment and empathetic understanding in response to Hempel. RELEVANCE: Dray's rational-explanation model is the principal analytic alternative to both Hempelian positivism and speculative teleology. By grounding historical understanding in practical rationality rather than either covering laws or teleological forces, Dray stakes out a distinctively analytic "critical" position. His critique of Hempel directly bears on whether teleological explanations (as in Kant's purposive nature or Hegel's rational history) can be philosophically legitimate. POSITION: Anti-positivist analytic account; defends rational explanation as irreducible to D-N covering-law model while rejecting speculative narrative.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, rational-explanation, covering-law, dray, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc"
}

@misc{walsh1951introduction,
    author = "Walsh, W. H.",
    title = "An Introduction to Philosophy of History",
    year = "1951",
    address = "London",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Walsh introduces the now-canonical distinction between "speculative" (or "substantive") and "critical" philosophy of history. Speculative philosophy -- Hegel, Marx, Toynbee -- asks what history means as a whole and claims to discern its direction or goal; critical philosophy asks second-order epistemological questions about how historians know, explain, and organize their material. Walsh defends the critical approach as philosophically legitimate and dismisses speculative philosophy as overreaching. He also develops the concept of "colligation" -- the organizing of historical events under a "colligatory concept" (e.g., "The Renaissance") as the basic unit of historical understanding -- as an alternative to covering-law explanation. RELEVANCE: Walsh's book is the founding document of the analytic critical/speculative distinction. The very framing of the research project -- "critiques of the speculative tradition" -- depends on Walsh's conceptual division. His colligation concept provides an analytic account of how historical synthesis works that is neither nomological nor teleologically speculative. POSITION: Foundational analytic establishment of the critical/speculative distinction; origin of the dominant professional framing of philosophy of history in Anglophone thought.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, critical-speculative-distinction, colligation, walsh, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "The new translation from Oxford University Press is the first critical edition of Hegel’s Lectures on the History of Philosophy available in English. It supersedes in many respects the previous translation. The merit and value of the new translation can be best appreciated by comparing it with the older, less expensive version. Haldane’s and Simson’s work is not bad as a translation, but it is more than 100 years old, and its irremediable defect is that it is a translation of the second German edition, the least desirable edition of Hegel’s History of Philosophy Lectures. Since the Haldane translation has been very influential and widely reprinted, a word of explanation is in order. abstract\_source = {ndpr}, The second German edition of Hegel’s Lectures on the History of Philosophy in theWerke(1840-1844) was prepared by Michelet, in part as a response to criticisms made of his first edition (1833-6). Michelet’s first German edition is the richest in source materials, which include Hegel’s own lecture manuscripts, transcriptions and/or fair copies made by his students over a period of several years. However, in the first edition these disparate materials were not distinguished or identified for the reader. Michelet’s concern was to produce something like a book that appeared to issue from a single source, instead of a compilation of lectures delivered over a period of several years. Consequently Michelet freely mixed Hegel’s own lecture manuscripts, which are of higher historical critical value, with student transcripts. Further, Michelet did not bother to compare critically the divergent formulations in his sources, and did not critically evaluate the sources, especially the student transcripts. Michelet’s method was to interweave the several sources he had into a single text in such a way that the external juxtaposition of sources would not be apparent to the reader, so that the textseemedto issue as a whole from the spirit of the author in a single outpouring, i.e., as a unified book, not a compendium of several sources and lecture series. The result was that Michelet leveled the difference between Hegel’s notes and those of his students, and leveled the differences between the student transcriptions. The first German edition (1833-6) received severe criticism from many contemporaries who had attended Hegel’s lectures. The attempt to make the lecture material book-like was not successful and did violence to the material. For example, Johann Eduard Erdmann attributed to Michelet’s editorial treatment of material part of the blame for mistakes commonly attributed to Hegel. Criticisms like this led to a second German edition (1840-44). Michelet’s response to criticisms of the 1836 edition was a thorough revision that tried to make the material even more book-like with fewer repetitions and even more amalgamation of materials. He reworked the materials of the first edition by blending phrases and by eliminating long and repetitive passages. Precisely these editorial principles of blending texts and expressions into a single editorially constructed narrative are what today cause the second German edition to be regarded as unsuccessful. For Michelet’s revisions in the second edition were not the result of new and better source materials, or of a critical differentiation of Hegel’s lecture manuscripts from student transcripts, but merely an editorial reworking of the earlier edition to make it appear more like a book from a single author. The Haldane-Simson English translation translates the least valuable second German edition (1840-44). In the 1940’s there were efforts to produce a German critical edition by Hoffmeister. Hoffmeister separates Hegel’s manuscript material from the student transcripts. But Hoffmeister’s work remains incomplete and inconsistently implemented. The new German critical edition is intended as a replacement for Hoffmeister’s edition. We are confronted by the paradox that Michelet, who could draw upon his living memory of Hegel’s lectures he attended in 1823-4, produced not one but two editorially constructed texts that blurred and leveled the differences of his source materials. On the other hand, our contemporary German editors, who obviously did not attend Hegel’s lectures and whose understanding of Hegel’s thought was shaped and mediated by Michelet’s edition, have through critical comparisons and text-critical methods tried to distinguish what Michelet amalgamated, and by drawing upon fewer but still extant transcripts have tried to evaluate the sources and to produce less misleading and more accurate texts of those lectures than did Michelet. But these editors also concede that there are limits to what modern scholarship can achieve: The project of producing a critical edition has been frustrated by the loss of many of the original materials, including the loss of all of Hegel’s manuscripts. Because of the loss of these materials, no new edition can be produced following text and source critical methods that equals or surpasses the material richness of the original 1833-6 edition. Although it does not make use of or exhibit modern editorial practices, the first Michelet edition remains the most complete compilation of the source material available. The new German edition concentrates on the lectures from Hegel’s Berlin period (1819, 1820, 1825-6, 1827-8, 1829-30, and 1831) for which there are distinct lecture transcripts. The version of the lecture course the editors have selected as the lead text is the 1825-6 version. It was chosen for the simple, pragmatic reason that it is by far the best attested by five transcripts. This mutual attestation of the best available transcripts allows the editors to reconstruct the material comparatively with a high degree of authenticity. The advantage of focusing on one year’s lectures is that it provides a better glimpse of Hegel’s delivery of the history of philosophy in the course of a single academic term, and reconstitutes this lecture course in more reliable form than did Michelet. There is a drawback, namely the content of the lectures is somewhat reduced in comparison with the original Michelet edition (1833-6). But this is not a serious disadvantage or omission, for two reasons."
}

@misc{gallie1964philosophy,
    author = "Gallie, W. B.",
    title = "Philosophy and the Historical Understanding",
    year = "1964",
    address = "London",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Gallie argues that history is paradigmatically a "follow-able story" and that the capacity to follow historical narratives is a primitive cognitive achievement not reducible to subsumption under laws. He develops the concept of "essentially contested concepts" (elaborated in his 1956 article) to argue that many key historical and political concepts are inherently subject to reasonable disagreement -- an argument that complicates both positivist demands for definitional clarity and speculative claims to have discovered history's true meaning. RELEVANCE: Gallie's "followable story" argument and his account of narrative comprehension provide an analytic defense of narrative form that neither reduces history to science nor endorses speculative teleology. The essentially-contested-concepts thesis directly challenges the definitional certainty required by speculative schemes that claim to know what "freedom," "reason," or "progress" really amount to historically. POSITION: Analytic pro-narrative account; narrative comprehension as irreducible cognitive achievement; essentially-contested-concepts critique applies against speculative teleological certainties.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, narrative, essentially-contested-concepts, gallie, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = {Bibliographical references included in "Notes" (p. 227-231},
    abstract_source = "core"
}

@article{gallie1956contested,
    author = "Gallie, W. B.",
    title = "Essentially Contested Concepts",
    journal = "Proceedings of the Aristotelian Society",
    year = "1956",
    volume = "56",
    pages = "167--198",
    doi = "10.1093/aristotelian/56.1.167",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Gallie argues that a range of philosophical and political concepts -- "art," "democracy," "justice," "Christian life" -- are "essentially contested": their proper application is perpetually disputed in ways that are not merely verbal or resoluble by empirical inquiry. Such concepts have an internal appraisive character and serve to guide and organize contested practices; reasonable people following the concept's standards may nonetheless arrive at incompatible applications. RELEVANCE: Applied to philosophy of history, the essentially-contested-concepts thesis implies that master-concepts of speculative history -- "freedom," "reason," "progress," "Spirit" -- cannot be given the determinate content that speculative philosophers (Hegel above all) require. If these concepts are essentially contested, then Hegel's claim to have determined their true referent in history, and Kant's claim to discern history's purposive telos, involve a denial of the concept's contestedness. POSITION: Analytic conceptual analysis that destabilizes the definitional foundations of speculative philosophy of history without endorsing positivist reduction.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, essentially-contested-concepts, gallie, speculative-history, High",
    abstract = "IX.—Essentially Contested Concepts W. B. Gallie W. B. Gallie Search for other works by this author on: Oxford Academic Google Scholar Proceedings of the Aristotelian Society, Volume 56, Issue 1, 1 June 1956, Pages 167–198, https://doi.org/10.1093/aristotelian/56.1.167 Published: 21 July 2015",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{mink1970history,
    author = "Mink, Louis O.",
    title = "History and Fiction as Modes of Comprehension",
    journal = "New Literary History",
    year = "1970",
    volume = "1",
    doi = "10.2307/468271",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Mink distinguishes three modes of comprehension -- theoretical (subsuming under laws or generalizations), categorical (classifying by kinds), and configurational (grasping as a unity by following a narrative from start to finish). Narrative comprehension in history is irreducibly configurational: it does not add up component events but rather retroactively organizes them into a whole. This "synoptic" view cannot be restated as a series of propositions or explanations. RELEVANCE: Mink's configurational comprehension thesis directly challenges both the covering-law model (which reduces explanation to theoretical subsumption) and speculative philosophy of history (which claims to provide the ultimately configurational narrative of history as a whole). The impossibility of a truly total configuration -- since any narrative endpoint is contingent -- supports the analytic rejection of Hegelian and Kantian grand schemes. POSITION: Analytic narrativist alternative to covering-law model; configurational comprehension as the specific mode of historical understanding, which cannot be globalized into speculative narrative.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, narrative, configurational-comprehension, mink, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: pages",
    abstract = "PHILOSOPHERS have always betrayed a certain scorn for both history and romance. I knew the delicacy of enlivens the mind, said Descartes, explaining how he had liberated himself from the errors of the schools, and that famous deeds of history ennoble it. But in the end, he concluded, these are negligible merits, because fiction makes us imagine a number of events as possible which are really impossible, and even the most faithful histories, if they do not alter or embroider things to make them more worth reading, almost always omit the meanest and least illustrious circumstances, so the remainder is distorted.' This was Descartes's first and final word on all the tales and stories",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{mandelbaum1967note,
    author = "Mandelbaum, Maurice",
    title = "A Note on History as Narrative",
    journal = "History and Theory",
    year = "1967",
    volume = "6",
    doi = "10.2307/2504424",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Mandelbaum offers a critical response to narrativist accounts of history (Mink, Gallie), arguing that narrative is not the fundamental or unique form of historical understanding. He distinguishes between chronicle (temporal sequence), narrative (selected sequence with explanatory connections), and critical history (explanatory analysis), insisting that genuinely explanatory history depends on the latter rather than on narrative coherence. RELEVANCE: Mandelbaum's skepticism about narrative-as-cognitive-mode represents a strand within analytic philosophy of history that is skeptical of both positivist covering-law claims and the narrativist alternative -- useful for mapping the internal debates that define "critical" philosophy against speculative narrative. POSITION: Internal analytic critique of narrativism; defends explanatory-analytical history against both covering-law positivism and narrative-first accounts.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, narrative, mandelbaum, historical-explanation, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: pages",
    abstract = "Among philosophers of it has recently been claimed with increasing frequency that if we are to understand what historians actually do, we should examine what features are characteristic of narratives, and should consider the manner in which a narrative may be said to explain the events included within it. As examples of this view Gallie, Danto, and Morton White may be cited.' Earlier forms of a similar tendency are to be found in Dray's continuous series model of explanation, in W. H. Walsh's account of as significant narrative, and in certain aspects of the theories of Croce and of Collingwood.2 To be sure, the positions of Gallie, of Danto, and of White differ from one another in various respects, and even more important differences exist between their views and those of Croce and of Collingwood. I shall not attempt to deal with these differences; what is here of concern to me is what I take to be a characteristic mark of recent theoretical writings concerning historiography. In the present note I wish to challenge the belief that historical inquiry and historical writing are essentially matters of constructing stories, narratives, or connected chronicles -terms, which, in the present context, I shall take as designating a single concept or a cluster of inseparable concepts. My argument will not be based on the fact that there are instances of historical inquiry which fail to conform to the characterization of as narrative; for example, that there are monographic studies which are not organized as narrative structures. I shall not rely on that argument since an opponent might claim that such studies are merely adjuncts to history proper, rather than being instances of it. My",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@misc{mandelbaum1977anatomy,
    author = "Mandelbaum, Maurice",
    title = "The Anatomy of Historical Knowledge",
    year = "1977",
    address = "Baltimore",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Mandelbaum provides a systematic analytic epistemology of historical knowledge, examining the nature of historical facts, the basis of causal claims in history, the role of generalization, and the structure of historical narratives. He defends the objectivity of historical knowledge against relativist and narrativist challenges while denying that history requires or produces covering-law explanations. RELEVANCE: As the most systematic analytic epistemology of history by the mid-1970s, this work represents the mature form of "critical" philosophy of history -- the project Walsh inaugurated. Its rejection of both positivist covering-law claims and speculative-narrativist claims provides important context for the research project's account of what the analytic mainstream considered legitimate versus confused. POSITION: Mature analytic epistemology of history; systematic defense of the critical tradition against both positivism and speculative narrative.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, historical-knowledge, epistemology, mandelbaum, Medium, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = "Originally published in 1977. In this major work, an overview of the structure of historical writing, Maurice Mandelbaum clarifies some of the problems concerning the nature of history as a discipline, of what constitutes explanation in history, and whether historical knowledge is as reliable as other forms of knowledge. The work is divided into three parts. The first part provides an analytic account of different types of historical inquiry. The second treats at length the nature of causal explanation in everyday life and in science and considers the relation between causes and laws. The final part analyzes the concept of objectivity and estimates both the extent to which the inquiries of historians can be said to be objective and the limits of that objectivity in some types of historical accounts",
    abstract_source = "core"
}

@misc{popper1957poverty,
    author = "Popper, Karl R.",
    title = "The Poverty of Historicism",
    year = "1957",
    address = "London",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Popper argues that "historicism" -- the view that history develops according to discoverable laws that allow prediction of its future course -- is scientifically and logically indefensible. His central argument is that the growth of knowledge cannot be predicted (since we cannot know now what we will know in the future), and since history is causally dependent on knowledge growth, historical development is in principle unpredictable. Popper identifies Hegel and Marx as the primary targets. RELEVANCE: Popper's critique of historicism is one of the most widely read analytic arguments against speculative philosophy of history. His demonstration that historical prediction is impossible in principle (not merely in practice) directly challenges Kantian and Hegelian teleology, which requires that history's direction can be discerned. The Poverty of Historicism helped define the analytic professional consensus against speculative history. POSITION: Anti-historicist analytic argument; demonstrates logical impossibility of historical prediction, thus refuting speculative teleology from a philosophy-of-science perspective.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, historicism, popper, speculative-history, High, METADATA\_CLEANED: publisher, type:@book->@misc",
    abstract = {Behind this catchy title lies a wealth of thought-provoking and original analysis which aims at clarifying the central issues of classical Cynicism from its antecedents. 'Cynicism', 'cynics' and 'cynical' are of course familiar enough words, but Desmond is keen to dispel the erroneous meanings they have accrued over the centuries and to go back to the genuine views, which, as he argues persuasively, to this day have lost little of their value. His use of a broad range of sources provides a firm basis for the analysis with a fresh look at familiar passages from Hesiod, Aristophanes, Xenophon, Plato, and Thucidydes (although one reviewer has already pointed out that the selection is still predominantly from the intellectual elite). The overall argument aims to show that Cynics were making use of, or perhaps tapping into, traditional sensibilities and opinions in Greek society regarding wealth and poverty. The Cynics developed a profile that denied wealth an elevated place and instead they sang the praise of poverty. Desmond shows that this position is not new nor completely marginal, but original and of universal appeal across the centuries. abstract\_source = {ndpr}, Desmond starts by saying that he wants to brush aside the confusion that he sees among interpretations of the ancient Cynics in which they have become regarded as marginal among Greek schools of thought. This position, which we may label the 'controversial thesis', is combined with a source-analysis that manages to connect the proto-Cynical sentiments with what he considers the central concept of the Cynics: the rejection of wealth. His approach is refreshing in that he proposes to uncover the general Greek ideas on wealth and poverty and how these somehow became the focal point of a small group of social critics typical for a new urban environment. On the follow-up of his thesis I demur, because the fundamental point that Desmond tries to develop, namely that Cynics are not as marginal as has traditionally been claimed, seems to be based on a particular understanding of 'marginal'; but his argument does not show clearly how popular their views may have been. Unless I am misreading him completely, the assumption seems to be that uncovering a broader perception of poverty (how wide-spread this view was remains a question, given the literary sources used) and establishing a link with the Cynic view should mean that they were less marginal. I think this reverses the actual process: popular views were taken up by the Cynics, not vice versa. Desmond should have clarified this concept of marginality, as it seems that the Cynic view on poverty, no matter how close to popular opinion, was in fact still marginal in classical Athens as a result of the methods used for preaching it: for instance, the 'shock therapy' that Diogenes is said to have used (obscenity, paradox, poor hygiene) would effectively marginalize him socially and hence philosophically. There is in addition the question whether we can take all these sources at face value: paying lip service to the praise of poverty is one thing, really believing in it another. By rejecting the reading of all asceticism as necessarilyreligious, Desmond paves the way for discussing more appropriate aspects in Cynicism, which he labels "social, military and intellectual asceticism" (19) each of which becomes the central theme of one chapter in the book (chs. 2, 3, and 4 respectively). Thus he maps out his program in accordance with what he sees as the real tenets of Cynicism (self-sufficiency, hard work [in Greek:ponos], and asceticism as major factors in the internalization of values, 30). Chapter 1 (by far the longest, 27-103) aims to show that a broader definition of wealth and poverty can help to throw light on the connection with popular beliefs by focussing on two paradoxes: (1) poverty is wealth, (2) idleness is work. The procedure is a sensible one: analysis of the terms 'poverty' and 'wealth' in terms of quality and quantity, types of wealth, and views on wealth shows the range of meanings. Desmond homes in on how the Greeks value humans as a major asset of the polis (35). Such judgements already signal that a more internal notion of wealth began to emerge in Classical Greece even though hard work is traditionally seen as the 'primary cause of wealth' (41), and injustice also features in Hesiod's work (Desmond's comment that Hesiod's dichotomy 'looks forward' [42] to Aristotle's discussion of wealth strikes me as unfortunate and potentially misleading). Concurrent phenomena such asleitourgia('burden of wealth', 55) and luxuries ('benefit of wealth', 61) help complete the picture of widely held views, including chestnuts such as the story of Croesus and Solon (63), to show that philosophical objections to luxury were not just the domain of intellectuals. The third, and more interesting, notion Desmonds looks at is leisure as a possible benefit of wealth. The traditional view would be that the rich enjoy more leisure. Yet the foregoing arguments onleitourgiaand luxuries have shown that these can be a burden as obligation, as a cause for concern (theft), and as distracting elements in one's daily activities. The Cynics would hold that the poor man has more leisure, since he is free from such obligations, possessions and external cares (67). The work ethic is another important aspect Desmond brings in, making good use of texts which provide a background to the economic situation of the time, as well as to the 'industrious optimism' found from Hesiod onward. Starting from the farmer's limited wealth (and a low expectation to acquire it -- "archaic pessimism", 74) the argument tries to show that wealth was viewed as something out of one's control (Desmond highlights the Greek word for "wealthy",eutuches, which also means "fortunate", 70). The second paradox concerns the cynics' idleness as a kind of badge of honor: how can one realistically defend the position that "idleness is work"? It links up with the argument about basic needs which are said to be limited and to require only limited work. The provocative formulation of the paradox uses 'idleness' in the traditional absolute sense (absence of work with a hint of laziness) and combines it in the paradoxical phrase equating it to 'work'. Desmond goes on to suggest that the new 'imperialist' work-ethic emerging in Athens brings on an increased need beyond the basic ones, commensurate with the higher stakes and greaterponoi. This imperial optimism he claims is part of the 5thc. rhetoric as exemplified in the Athenian funeral orations (78). One can imagine a certain euphoria is involved here after the unexpected victories over the Persians, but should we take this kind of wishful thinking literally, I wonder? At the same time, Desmond is of course right that Alexander can be seen as an extension of this kind of optimism and as the prime example of insatiable ambitions. Yet we find much speculation (and he knows it; see, e.g., his justification by using "extrapolation" 79). For Hellenistic times one could then expect philosophical ambitions matching the political ones, if only because the world was 'expanding' and human ambitions with it. The idea of limits and their absence, Desmond suggests, are transferred to ideas about needs for body and soul, finite and infinite respectively. It is clearly Socrates who is presented by Plato as one of the earliest to recommend toil for the soul (cf. XenophonMemorabilia2.1.17), playing on the internal / external opposition, making the philosophical life into one of 'toil'. In contrast with the highly intellectual focus of Plato's and Aristotle's philosophies (87-95), the Cynics can be seen to emphasize "work" (ponos) as both physical and mental, in which everything is geared to conquering oneself (instead of Sicily as Athens wanted to, but failed). They claim to be specialists in virtue through asceticism (fromaskesismeaning "exercise, training, hard work"), which they believe "can overcome everything". The chapter concludes by stating that the Cynics were the synthesizers of the inequalities of the fourth century: hence the emphasis on self-sufficiency which will make us into unassailable strongholds, while dispositions such as povertyand powerlessness (traditionally viewed as weaknesses) can be reconfigured into strengths.}
}

@book{fain1970between,
    author = "Fain, Haskell",
    title = "Between Philosophy and History: The Resurrection of Speculative Philosophy of History within the Analytic Tradition",
    year = "1970",
    publisher = "Princeton University Press",
    address = "Princeton",
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Fain argues against the standard analytic dismissal of speculative philosophy of history, contending that speculative concerns -- with the overall meaning of history, with historical progress, with the philosophical significance of large historical patterns -- are not logically confused but philosophically legitimate. He attempts a partial rehabilitation of speculative philosophy within an analytic framework, challenging Walsh's sharp critical/speculative distinction. RELEVANCE: Fain's book is the principal internal analytic challenge to the critical/speculative distinction. For the research project it is essential as representing the minority position that speculative philosophy of history -- including concerns central to Hegel and Kant -- can survive analytic scrutiny. Understanding the critical/speculative distinction requires acknowledging that it was not universally accepted even within analytic philosophy. POSITION: Pro-speculative minority view within analytic philosophy of history; challenges the professional consensus established by Walsh, Hempel, and Danto.",
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, speculative-history, critical-speculative-distinction, fain, Medium",
    abstract = "In 2019, Jürgen Habermas published with Suhrkamp Press his 1700-plus pageAuch eine Geschichte der Philosophie. The work appeared in two volumes, subtitled: (1)The Occidental Constellation of Faith and Knowledgeand (2)Rational Freedom: Traces of Discourses on Faith and Reason. In the English language,Also a History of Philosophy(AHP), masterfully translated by Ciarin Cronin, is published by Polity Press in three volumes: (1)The Project of Genealogy of Postmetaphysical Thinking(2022); (2)The Occidental Constellation of Faith and Knowledge(2024); and (3)Rational Freedom. Traces of the Discourse on Faith and Knowledge(2025). abstract\_source = {ndpr}, The review that follows focuses on Volume III of the English translation. First, I provide an overview of some of the work’s more important claims. Then I pose some questions, principally about the work’s systematic philosophic claims but also, briefly, about its status as a history of philosophy. As the title indicates, the work is a history of philosophy, one that traces the relationship of faith and reason as it played out historically, primarily in Western thought. As the title also implies, however, the work is more than just a history of philosophy, distinctive though that itself is. It is also and perhaps most significantly a defense of the notion of postmetaphysical reason, which has been a central focus of Habermas’s work for the past thirty years or so. AHP is distinctive, however, in that it presents that defense via a genealogical reconstruction of the evolution of philosophical thought. Central to this “genealogy of postmetaphysical thinking” is an effort to redeem and recast for contemporary thought crucial insights and assumptions introduced to human civilization during what, following Karl Jaspers, Habermas calls the Axial Age. Occurring in the period between 800 and 200 BCE, the Axial Age reflected the ascendance of the major world religions, including Confucianism, Taoism, Hinduism, Buddhism, and Judaism, as well as classical Greek philosophy. Common to all these movements was an affirmation of principles and values central to the subsequent development principally of Western thought and culture. Among others, these included the idea of objective validity, the principle of the worth and equality of all individuals, a commitment to the welfare of humankind, and an expectation that human affairs be governed by binding norms and impartial standards of justice and morality. Over 1700 pages, Habermas presents the ensuing interplay of faith and reason as a historical “learning process”, in which the principles and values bequeathed to humanity by the Axial Age are further clarified while gradually purged of the metaphysical and theological detritus that has impeded their proper articulation. Resulting from this process of learning are core elements of Habermas’s idea of postmetaphysical thinking. At issue, in particular, is a notion of rationality committed to norms of objective validity, impartial truth, human equality and historical progress, where those norms are deemed to express, not an absolute authority to which humans are vertically related, but the immanent, horizontally directed, open-ended and fallibilistically conceived practices through which historically situated and “communicatively socialized” individuals shape and reshape the conditions of their shared existence. Volume III is itself distinctive, as here the genealogy first details the historical emergence of postmetaphysical thinking. Habermas’s focus is on Hume and Kant, each of whom advances a philosophy that rejects the metaphysical assumptions that had accompanied the preceding discourse on faith and knowledge. They do so in diverse ways, however. Hume does so by dismissing or “deconstructing” the religious-theological legacy, while Kant reappropriates or “reconstructs” it. Habermas’s preference is decidedly for the Kantian option. Indeed, the task of Volume III, as its subtitle indicates, is to clarify for postmetaphysical thinking the concept of “rational freedom” (vernünftige Freiheit) initially elaborated by Kant. That Habermas nonetheless focuses on Hume is attributable not only to the fact that Kant develops his position in response to that of Hume but also that the latter underwrites the scientistic naturalism that today has spawned the self-regulating social systems against which, in the text, he seeks to “encourage” the idea of rational freedom. Habermas understands rational freedom specifically in terms of Kant’s idea of practical self-legislation. He dubs this idea “one of the few major innovations in the history philosophy” (262), yet presents it, consistent with the effort to discern “traces of the discourse on faith and knowledge”, as rearticulating assumptions that have long informed religious traditions. Thus, while a comprehensive understanding of moral obligation was historically construed in terms of divinely ordained commands, Kant’s understanding of it appeals to universal duties that are humanly self-imposed. Similarly, while Kant continues to espouse the notion, central also to religions, that thought might provide human beings with holistic world orientation, he does so, not by seeking to integrate individuals into a preexisting order of being, but by referencing a world order reflective of the constitutive activity of human beings themselves."
}

@article{beaney2016historiography,
    author = "Beaney, Michael",
    title = "Historiography, Philosophy of History and the Historical Turn in Analytic Philosophy",
    journal = "Journal of the Philosophy of History",
    year = "2016",
    volume = "10",
    pages = "211--234",
    doi = "10.1163/18722636-12341321",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Beaney surveys the relationship between the history of analytic philosophy and philosophy of history, arguing that the "historical turn" in recent analytic philosophy -- increased interest in the history of philosophy as philosophically relevant -- has important methodological implications for how analytic historians of philosophy understand their own practice. He charts the emergence and decline of analytic philosophy of history as a sub-discipline, identifying why it became eclipsed by hermeneutic and narrativist approaches after the 1970s. RELEVANCE: This retrospective account of analytic philosophy of history's trajectory -- its emergence in the 1940s--60s, dominance through the 1970s, and subsequent marginalization -- provides valuable historiographical framing for the research project's account of how the analytic critique became the professional standard and what led to its eclipse. POSITION: Retrospective historiographical account of analytic philosophy of history as a sub-discipline; traces the rise and decline of the critical tradition.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, historiography, historical-turn, beaney, Medium",
    abstract = "This article has three main interconnected aims. First, I illustrate the historiographical conceptions of three early analytic philosophers: Frege, Russell and Wittgenstein. Second, I consider some of the historiographical debates that have been generated by the recent historical turn in analytic philosophy, looking at the work of Scott Soames and Hans-Johann Glock, in particular. Third, I discuss Arthur Danto’s Analytic Philosophy of History , published 50 years ago, and argue for a reinvigorated analytic philosophy of history.",
    abstract_source = "openalex"
}

@article{uebel2019acts,
    author = "Uebel, Thomas",
    title = "Acts, Events, and Stories. On the History of {Danto's} Compatibilist Narrativism",
    journal = "Journal of the Philosophy of History",
    year = "2019",
    volume = "14",
    pages = "47--79",
    doi = "10.1163/18722636-12341412",
    note = {CORE ARGUMENT: Uebel reconstructs the developmental trajectory of Danto's Analytical Philosophy of History (1965) from his unpublished PhD dissertation (1952) through early papers, showing that Danto's position shifted away from historical idealism toward what Uebel calls "compatibilist narrativism" -- the view that deductive-nomological and narrative explanations are logically compatible yet employ incommensurable schemata. Uebel argues this trajectory runs contrary to interpretations that see Danto as moving smoothly from philosophy of science toward postmodern aesthetics. RELEVANCE: This detailed historical reconstruction of Danto's argument is essential for understanding the precise nature of the analytic critique of speculative history in Danto's work. By showing that Danto's 1965 position was a "reasoned retreat" from idealism rather than an anticipation of postmodernism, Uebel clarifies what the analytic critique actually amounts to and how it differs from both positivism and narrativism. POSITION: Historical reconstruction of Danto's philosophical development; clarifies the analytic distinctiveness of his critique of speculative history.},
    keywords = "analytic-philosophy-of-history, danto, narrative-sentences, compatibilist-narrativism, Medium",
    abstract = "abstract\_source = {s2}, The response given to C.G. Hempel’s well-known challenge by Arthur Danto in his Analytical Philosophy of History of 1965 – that deductive-nomological and narrative explanations are logically compatible yet employ incommensurable schemata – is here investigated from a historical perspective. It is shown that the developmental trajectory that emerges from an analysis of Danto’s previous writings – including not only a forgotten paper of 1958 but also his PhD dissertation of 1952 – contains distinctive step-changes with publications of 1953 and 1956 still prior to that of 1958–59 which enabled his subsequent discovery of narrative sentences. It is also argued that Danto’s developmental trajectory runs contrary to that presumed by some prominent commentators. Analytical History of Philosophy was not the midpoint of his ascent from mainstream philosopher of science to high priest of postmodern aesthetics, but represents a reasoned retreat from his early historical idealism."
}

@book{lyotard1984postmodern,
  author    = {Lyotard, Jean-Fran\c{c}ois},
  title     = {The Postmodern Condition: A Report on Knowledge},
  year      = {1984},
  publisher = {University of Minnesota Press},
  address   = {Minneapolis},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lyotard argues that "postmodernity" is defined by
  "incredulity towards metanarratives" -- the grand legitimating stories
  of modernity such as Hegel's dialectic of Spirit and Kant's narrative
  of rational emancipation. In these speculative regimes, local language
  games are subordinated to a single overarching legitimation, which
  Lyotard identifies as an exercise of epistemic and political violence.
  The collapse of grand narratives does not mean relativism but calls for
  "paralogical" forms of legitimation attending to incommensurable
  language games.

  RELEVANCE: This is the text that defined the postmodern critique of
  speculative teleology. Lyotard's specific argument that Hegel's
  speculative dialectic is a "grand narrative" whose totalising logic
  suppresses difference is directly relevant to any evaluation of the
  speculative tradition in philosophy of history. The text provides the
  philosophical vocabulary for the critique examined across this entire
  domain.

  POSITION: Postmodern anti-metanarrative; critique of Hegelian
  speculative legitimation. Cross-domain: Lyotard's engagement with
  Kant's "Enthusiasm" and philosophy of history (his 1986 work on
  Kant's critique of history complements this text).
  },
  keywords = {lyotard, metanarrative, postmodern-condition, grand-narrative, hegel-critique, teleology-critique, High, poststructuralism-postmodernism},
  abstract = {More readily perhaps than most volumes of collected works, this volume, the first of three from Adolf Grünbaum, can be read with profit in two quite different ways. It can, and should, be read first for what it is, a collection of ten of Grünbaum's best-known essays on scientific "rationality" (three essays), "determinism and the human condition" (three as well), and "theological interpretations of twentieth-century physical cosmologies" (four essays, including "The Poverty of Theistic Cosmology," a 50-pagetour de force). Forthcoming second and third volumes will assemble Grünbaum's most significant papers on the philosophy of physics and the philosophy (and particularly the methodology) of psychoanalysis respectively, two areas of research shaped in no small part by those very papers and for which Grünbaum is, arguably, better known. But it is a very good thing that we have in this initial volume Grünbaum taking up topics of more general appeal, and a wide range of them to boot. For as a result, the many who lack the special training (or interest) in space-time or psychoanalysis are nevertheless afforded a focused and unobstructed view of a first-rate philosophical mind in action.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

Grünbaum's topics are wide-ranging, but not haphazard. Grünbaum is at pains, for example, to defend inductivism -- the notion that evidence that conforms to a hypothesis (under certain conditions) gives us reason to believe that hypothesis -- against its critics. He argues at great length that determinism (or, for that matter, indeterminism) is no threat to human free will, nor therefore to any science which attempts to study the "inner life of free choice." Grünbaum argues at greater length that theism is undeserving of the high moral ground it has appropriated, and that theism (like atheism) is "morally sterile," although atheism does fit comfortably within a secular humanism, which may in turn provide moral imperatives. In four successive essays, Grünbaum addresses several facets of the question of whether our most current understanding of the origin and nature of the cosmos warrants belief in the existence of a omniscient, omnipotent, and all-loving deity, taking up for example in the first of these the question of why anything exists at all, or, more carefully, why all the things that exist and yetmight have not existedexist, as opposed to nothingthat might not have existedexisting. The claims of theists like Richard Swinburne and Philip Quinn that any satisfactory answer to this question requires theism depend, Grünbaum argues, on confusion and non sequiturs.

This last question Grünbaum dubs the "Primordial Existential Question" (p. 151), but in fact most of the questions Grünbaum takes up are primordial, albeit in the term's less grand sense. These essays concern existence, suffering, love, death, God, sex, passion, social activism, religion, evil, free will, and human nature, and, throughout, the capacity of reason to enable us to think clearly and correctly about these things. It is with these sorts of questions that philosophy begins, in the utterly prosaic sense that it is with these sorts of questions that many undergraduates enroll in Introduction to Philosophy. More generally, these are the sorts of questions that the non-philosophical public expects philosophers to be able to at least address, if not illuminate and answer.

Those who wisely applaud Grünbaum's attention to this broad range of philosophical topics will find much to praise in the approach he takes to them as well. The tenets of this approach, characteristic not just of Grünbaum but of generations of philosophers who look to, for example, Bertrand Russell, are something like the following: Knowing the true answer to the question at hand is of the greatest value. Logic is to be valued, and used, in the service of preserving truth. Clarity is of a piece with logic and is similarly prized, not just for its communicative function in truth-preservation but as a social marker of intellectual virtue (logic, after all, being sensitive only to the preservation of truth and exerting its force upon belief independently of an arguer's particular ends, is consequently of use only to those who seek nothing but the truth). And, lastly, the questions deemed worthy of one's attention are just those one encounters in the course of answering questions that confront all humans: what is this world, what are we in it, and how is it that we can know it?

Grünbaum doesn't exemplify this approach to philosophy so much as embody it. There is in every essay a certain care in definition, a recognizable laying-out of theses, and an even pace to the reasoning, reasoning which often depends upon nothing more intricate than modus ponens. The simple tools are, however, used with great skill. Consider Grünbaum's handling of the apparent incompatibility of holding a deterministic sociopolitical theory while also urging political action on behalf of the changes that, according to that very sociopolitical theory, are inevitable. If, for example, someone believes that a decline in protectionism, or a revolution of the proletariat, is inevitable, it might appear foolish at best for that person to lobby for protectionism or foment the revolution; sociopolitical determinism appears to undermine calls for political or socialaction. With particular clarity and force Grünbaum described the mistake in this line of reasoning in 1956:

the predictions made [by a sociopolitical theory] are predicated on the prior fulfillment of certain initial conditions which include the presence in that society of men who are dissatisfied with the existing state of affairs and are therefore actively seeking the future realization of the predicted social state. To ignore that the determinist rests his social prediction in part on the existence of the latter initial condition, just as much as a physicist makes a prediction of a thermal expansion conditional upon the presence of heat, is to commit the fallacy of equating determinism with fatalism. . . . Equally untenable is [the] claim that it is practically futile for determinists to weigh alternative modes of social organization with a view to optimizing their own social arrangements. For the determinist doesnotmaintain, in fatalist fashion, that the future state of society is independent of the decisions which men make in response to (i) facts (both physical and social), (ii) their owninterpretationof these facts . . . and (iii) their value objectives. (pp. 111-112; emphasis in original)

Determinism, far from being a threat to political and social activism, is a necessary condition of it.

The arguments and positions defended in this first volume are, on many occasions, not original to Grünbaum, nor of course does he claim they are. Readers are likely to find much more in the way of Grünbaum's distinct and original philosophical contributions in the subsequent volumes on space-time and psychoanalysis. This volume's considerable value is as a model of that philosophical approach mentioned above. As such, it will reward the attention given it by any professional or even aspiring philosopher, whatever her or his philosophical approach or inclination.},
}

@book{lyotard1988differend,
  author    = {Lyotard, Jean-Fran\c{c}ois},
  title     = {The Differend: Phrases in Dispute},
  year      = {1988},
  publisher = {University of Minnesota Press},
  address   = {Minneapolis},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lyotard develops the concept of the "differend" -- a
  wrong that cannot be expressed in the idiom of the dominant rule --
  to argue that after the Shoah there can be no recourse to Hegelian
  speculative teleology or Marxist teleological emancipation. The
  heterogeneity of phrase regimes cannot be resolved by appeal to a
  single tribunal of reason or a universal telos of history. Speculative
  philosophy of history is not merely false but structurally unjust
  in its suppression of incommensurable claims.

  RELEVANCE: This is Lyotard's most systematic philosophical elaboration
  of the anti-speculative position. Where The Postmodern Condition
  diagnoses the loss of metanarratives, The Differend explains why this
  loss is philosophically and ethically necessary. Essential for
  understanding the normative stakes of Lyotard's critique of Hegel.

  POSITION: Critique of speculative reason; philosophy of language games
  and incommensurability. Cross-domain: engages Kant's Critique of
  Judgment and the sublime as resources against Hegelian synthesis.
  },
  keywords = {lyotard, differend, phrase-regimes, incommensurability, speculative-history, High, poststructuralism-postmodernism, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{baudrillard1995simulacra,
  author    = {Baudrillard, Jean},
  title     = {Simulacra and Simulation},
  year      = {1995},
  publisher = {University of Michigan Press},
  address   = {Ann Arbor},
  doi       = {10.3998/mpub.9904},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Baudrillard argues that contemporary culture is
  characterized by the precession of simulacra -- the replacement of
  reality by models and signs that refer only to other signs. In this
  condition of "hyperreality," the real has been abolished: history, as
  a narrative of events grounded in reality, gives way to the simulation
  of history. The teleological order presupposed by speculative
  philosophies of history -- progress toward a rational end -- is
  dissolved when the referent (historical reality) itself disappears.

  RELEVANCE: Baudrillard's theory of simulation offers a distinct kind
  of anti-teleological argument: not that progress narratives are false
  or unjust, but that their very precondition (a real world that history
  tracks) has imploded. This "end of history" via simulation
  complements Lyotard's language-game argument and provides a
  materialist-semiological critique of speculative philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Simulacral dissolution of historical teleology; the
  "hyperreal" as post-historical condition.
  },
  keywords  = {baudrillard, simulation, hyperreality, end-of-history, anti-teleology, High, poststructuralism-postmodernism},
  abstract = {The transition from signs which dissimulate something to signs which dissimulate that there is nothing, marks the decisive turning point. The first implies a theology of truth and secrecy. The second inaugurates an age of simulacra and simulation, in which there is no longer any God to recognize his own, nor any last judgement to separate truth from false, the real from its artificial resurrection, since everything is already dead and risen in advance. Disneyland is a perfect model of all the entangled orders of simulation. The objective profile of the United States, then, may be traced throughout Disneyland, even down to the morphology of individuals and the crowd. The Disneyland imaginary is neither true nor false: it is a deterrence machine Set up in order to rejuvenate in reverse the fiction of the real.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{rorty1989contingency,
  author    = {Rorty, Richard},
  title     = {Contingency, Irony, and Solidarity},
  year      = {1989},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address   = {Cambridge},
  doi       = {10.1017/cbo9780511804397},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rorty argues for a thoroughgoing anti-foundationalism
  in philosophy and politics: there is no ahistorical standpoint from
  which to adjudicate between vocabularies or narratives, and Hegel's
  speculative dialectic is a failed attempt to provide such a
  standpoint. Rorty's "liberal ironist" is someone who recognizes the
  contingency of her own final vocabulary without abandoning solidarity.
  History is not the unfolding of reason (contra Hegel) but a series of
  redescriptions with no telos.

  RELEVANCE: Rorty's anti-teleological pragmatism represents the
  analytic-continental crossover critique of speculative philosophy of
  history. His reading of Hegel as a proto-pragmatist who then falls
  back into metaphysics is directly relevant to evaluating the
  speculative tradition. Cross-domain figure: connects poststructuralist
  themes with analytic anti-foundationalism.

  POSITION: Anti-foundationalist pragmatism; contingency as
  irreducible; critique of Hegelian teleology from within the
  Anglophone tradition.
  },
  keywords  = {rorty, contingency, anti-teleology, pragmatism, hegel-critique, cross-domain, High, poststructuralism-postmodernism},
  abstract = {In this 1989 book Rorty argues that thinkers such as Nietzsche, Freud, and Wittgenstein have enabled societies to see themselves as historical contingencies, rather than as expressions of underlying, ahistorical human nature or as realizations of suprahistorical goals. This ironic perspective on the human condition is valuable on a private level, although it cannot advance the social or political goals of liberalism. In fact Rorty believes that it is literature not philosophy that can do this, by promoting a genuine sense of human solidarity. A truly liberal culture, acutely aware of its own historical contingency, would fuse the private, individual freedom of the ironic, philosophical perspective with the public project of human solidarity as it is engendered through the insights and sensibilities of great writers. The book has a characteristically wide range of reference from philosophy through social theory to literary criticism. It confirms Rorty's status as a uniquely subtle theorist, whose writing will prove absorbing to academic and nonacademic readers alike.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{megill1985prophets,
  author    = {Megill, Allan},
  title     = {Prophets of Extremity: Nietzsche, Heidegger, Foucault, Derrida},
  year      = {1985},
  publisher = {University of California Press},
  doi       = {10.1525/9780520908376},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Megill provides a systematic intellectual history of
  how Nietzsche, Heidegger, Foucault, and Derrida constituted a common
  "aestheticist" or "extremist" challenge to the Enlightenment project
  and its philosophy of history. He argues that all four thinkers share
  a rejection of the "normal canons of rationality" and of historical
  teleology, substituting aesthetic criteria of judgment for epistemic
  ones. The book traces the common anti-developmental logic across these
  figures.

  RELEVANCE: This is the standard secondary reference for reading
  Nietzsche, Foucault, and Derrida together as critics of speculative
  philosophy of history. Megill's account of what these thinkers share
  and how they differ provides essential context for the research
  project's comparison of poststructuralist critiques.

  POSITION: Intellectual history of poststructuralist anti-rationalism;
  "prophets of extremity" thesis. Secondary overview covering all main
  figures in this domain.
  },
  keywords  = {megill, nietzsche, heidegger, foucault, derrida, anti-rationalism, secondary-overview, High, poststructuralism-postmodernism},
  abstract = {Preface Key to Abbreviations Introduction Part I: Friedrich Nietzsche as Aestheticist 1. Nietzsche and the Aesthetic 2. Nietzsche and Myth Part II: Martin Heidegger and the Idealism of Nostalgia 3. Heidegger and Crisis 4. Heidegger's Aestheticism Part III: Michel Foucalt and the Activism of Discourse 5. Foucault and Structuralism 6. Beyond Structuralism Part IV: On the Meaning of Jacques Derrida 7. The Deconstruction of Crisis 8. The Deconstruction of Art Conclusion Notes A Note on Texts Index},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{osullivan2024realism,
  author  = {O'Sullivan, Luke},
  title   = {Realism, Postmodernism and/as Metanarrative},
  journal = {Journal of the Philosophy of History},
  year    = {2024},
  volume  = {18},
  number  = {2},
  pages   = {223--233},
  doi     = {10.1163/18722636-12341529},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: O'Sullivan examines the philosophical relationship
  between "realism" and "metanarrative" in the philosophy of history,
  arguing that Lyotard's anti-metanarrative postmodernism is itself a
  form of philosophical realism. He tracks how Fukuyama's neo-Hegelian
  "end of history" thesis complicated Lyotard's claim that metanarratives
  had lost their legitimacy, and analyzes Derrida's response to Fukuyama
  in Specters of Marx as an alternative engagement with teleological
  history. He distinguishes "utopian" modern metanarratives from
  "realist" postmodern ones.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most directly relevant recent article for
  the research project: it analyzes Lyotard's anti-metanarrative
  position against the background of speculative teleology (Hegel,
  Fukuyama) and examines Derrida's alternative. It situates the
  postmodern critique precisely within the philosophy of history debate.

  POSITION: Revisionist reading of postmodern anti-metanarrative as
  a species of philosophical realism; engages with Hegelian teleology
  and Fukuyama directly.
  },
  keywords  = {lyotard, derrida, metanarrative, realism, fukuyama, philosophy-of-history, High, poststructuralism-postmodernism},
  abstract = {
  abstract_source = {s2},
The question of how realism and metanarratives are related in the philosophy of history does not seem to have been widely discussed. Whereas there are distinct philosophical and political senses of ‘realism’, contrasting with ‘idealism’ and ‘utopianism’ respectively, ‘metanarrative’ has a singular meaning based on Jean-François Lyotard’s sceptical definition of postmodernism as “incredulity towards metanarratives”. Lyotard defined metanarratives as philosophies of history that serve some legitimatory function, but claimed that their importance was waning. From this point of view, postmodernism can be described as a species of philosophical ‘realism’. But the appearance of Francis Fukuyama’s ‘end of history’ thesis, which explicitly presented itself as a neo-Hegelian critique of political realism, suggested that Lyotard had underestimated the enduring power of metanarrative insofar as it served the interests of the American liberal-democratic capitalist order. For Jacques Derrida, Fukuyama’s work thus underlined the importance of breaking with what he called ‘onto-theological’ visions of history, although Derrida himself could be seen as authoring a metanarrative in the service of European social democracy. But if so, Derrida’s approach to metanarratives was very different in kind to contemporary religious and nationalistic versions. One way to resolve the difficulty is to make a distinction between modern ‘utopian’ and postmodern ‘realist’ versions of metanarratives.},
}

@article{manley2025disunity,
  author  = {Manley, Robin},
  title   = {The Disunity in Genealogy: {Foucault}'s Anti-{Nietzschean} Reading of History},
  journal = {Theory, Culture \& Society},
  year    = {2025},
  volume  = {42},
  number  = {5},
  pages   = {75--92},
  doi     = {10.1177/02632764251319026},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Contrary to the standard reading, Manley argues that
  Foucault's genealogy is incompatible with Nietzsche's philosophy of
  history rather than a faithful development of it. Through close reading
  of Foucault's "Nietzsche, Genealogy, History" alongside Nietzsche's
  second Untimely Meditation, Manley shows that Foucault presents an
  anti-metaphysical Nietzsche suspicious of teleological origins, whereas
  Nietzsche's own essay insists on the necessity of ahistorical
  "monumental" and "critical" history as antidotes to historicism.
  Foucault's anti-teleological reading of Nietzsche is itself selective.

  RELEVANCE: This article complicates the common poststructuralist
  genealogy (Nietzsche -- Foucault) by showing that Foucault's
  anti-teleological reading of Nietzsche involves a distortion. This is
  important for evaluating what is distinctively "Nietzschean" versus
  "Foucauldian" in the poststructuralist critique of speculative history.

  POSITION: Revisionist reading; argues Nietzsche is an alternative to,
  not precursor of, Foucauldian genealogy.
  },
  keywords  = {foucault, nietzsche, genealogy, anti-teleology, effective-history, High, poststructuralism-postmodernism},
  abstract = {Contemporary critical scholarship takes Foucault’s genealogical work as a paradigm, and it is widely recognized that Foucault himself took Nietzsche as a methodological exemplar. Notably, Foucault’s landmark methodological text – ‘Nietzsche, Genealogy, History’ – is framed as a faithful exposition of Nietzsche. Contrary to Foucault’s framing, however, this article argues that his account of genealogy is incompatible with and anathema to Nietzsche’s philosophy of history. Through a close reading of Foucault’s essay alongside Nietzsche’s second Unfashionable Observation, it seeks to demonstrate that Foucault’s account of genealogy is premised on an inadequately one-sided reading of Nietzsche’s more dialectical account of history and the ahistorical. Whereas Foucault presents an anti-metaphysical Nietzsche suspicious of teleological origins and ahistorical forms, Nietzsche’s essay in fact insists on the necessity of such figures as an antidote to historicism. Reading Nietzsche against Foucault, this article investigates the conceptual foundations of the contemporary genealogical method, suggesting that Nietzsche may present an alternative – rather than precursor – to the Foucauldian paradigm.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{garruzzo2025history,
  author  = {Garruzzo, Anthony},
  title   = {History and the Will to Power: {Foucault} and {Nietzsche} on Genealogy},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year    = {2025},
  volume  = {34},
  number  = {1},
  pages   = {191--205},
  doi     = {10.1111/ejop.70010},
  note    = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Against normatively-oriented accounts of genealogy,
  Garruzzo argues that genealogy for both Nietzsche and Foucault is
  defined by its investigation of the contingent origins of institutions
  within regimes of power -- specifically, through the lens of the will
  to power. Genealogy's critical contribution is not normative insight
  but "tactical knowledge": understanding how power has been exercised
  historically to better oppose it in the present. Any explicitly
  teleological "genealogy" is a contradiction in terms.

  RELEVANCE: This article clarifies the anti-teleological logic built
  into genealogy as a historical method: genealogy cannot be
  "normatively" teleological because its entire point is to expose the
  contingency and power-laden origins of normative claims. This directly
  addresses the question of whether genealogy constitutes a credible
  alternative to speculative history.

  POSITION: Reconstructive account of Nietzsche-Foucault genealogy as
  essentially anti-teleological tactical knowledge.
  },
  keywords  = {foucault, nietzsche, genealogy, will-to-power, anti-teleology, Medium, poststructuralism-postmodernism},
  abstract = {In recent years, there has been a revival of interest in genealogy among social and political philosophers. I argue, however, that this growing literature has tended to obscure what distinguishes genealogy as an approach to the interpretation of history. Any normative evaluation of something that appeals to an account of its origins has come to be called a “genealogy,” even if this account is explicitly hypothetical or unabashedly teleological. But a genealogy, according to Friedrich Nietzsche and Michel Foucault, is not merely an account of origins: it is an account of the contingent origins of the integration of an institution, practice, or concept within a regime of power and control. In other words, genealogy investigates an institution, practice, or concept through the lens of what Nietzsche calls “the will to power.” This paper aims to reconstruct the conception of the will to power Foucault inherits from Nietzsche in a way that explains why investigations into struggles for power from the past can provide insight into the strategies used for exercising and maintaining power in the present. The critical contribution of genealogy, I argue, is not normative insight but tactical knowledge: to oppose something successfully, first you must know your enemy.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

