  contemporary defense of why causal-law models are inadequate for historical
  understanding, without abandoning the possibility of historical knowledge.

  POSITION: Neo-Diltheyan; mediates between analytic philosophy of science
  and the continental hermeneutic tradition.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Verstehen, Dilthey, explanation, human-sciences, Medium},
  abstract = {Wilhelm Dilthey's essay of 1894, Ideas Concerning a Descriptive and Analytical Psychology, is the locus classicus for the distinction between `understanding' and `explanation', or Verstehen and Erklaren, in the 19th-century German tradition of hermeneutics and the Geistes-wissenschaften. This article discusses the distinction Dilthey draws there between `explanatory' psychology, based on subsumption of the behaviour of individuals under general laws, and `interpretive', or `descriptive and analytical', psychology, based on disclosure of the uniqueness of individual case-histories. It defends his conception against the objections of the Neo-Kantian philosophers Wilhelm Windelband and Heinrich Rickert and the experimental psychologist Hermann Ebbinghaus, as well as neo-positivist writers such as Theodore Abel. The article also argues more generally that Dilthey's dichotomy of `spirit' and `nature' still articulates a fundamental methodological difference between the sciences, despite our contemporary recognition of the importance of interpretation in both the natural and human sciences.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{doro2000collingwood,
  author = {D'Oro, Giuseppina},
  title = {Collingwood on Re-Enactment and the Identity of Thought},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2000},
  volume = {38},
  number = {1},
  pages = {87--101},
  doi = {10.1353/hph.2005.0091},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Collingwood's re-enactment doctrine is not a
  form of psychological empathy but a logical thesis about the identity of
  thought across time: the historian re-enacts the same thought as the past
  agent, not a psychologically similar thought. This makes re-enactment
  metaphysically distinctive and defends it against the objection that it
  collapses into imaginative projection.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the sharpest philosophical defense of Collingwood's
  central methodological claim, which is the pivot of his anti-speculative
  position: if historical understanding consists in re-enacting individual
  thoughts, then no general pattern, law, or teleology can be the primary
  object of history. Addresses the project's question about the viability of
  re-enactment as an alternative to speculative history.

  POSITION: Idealist hermeneutics; logical rather than psychological reading
  of Collingwood; defends the autonomy of historical understanding.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Collingwood, re-enactment, historical-understanding, idealism, High},
  abstract = {Collingwood's The Idea of History is often discussed in the context of the issue of the reducibility/non-reducibility of explanations in the social sciences to explanations in the natural sciences. In the 1950s and 60s, following the publication of Hempel's influential article, "The Function of General Laws in History,"1 many looked at Collingwood's defence of the autonomy of history as providing the appropriate conceptual resources to counter Hempel's claim that explanations in history and in the natural sciences share the same logical structure. Collingwood was recruited as an ally in a number of debates in analytical philosophy of history with a view to clarifying the nature of appropriately historical explanations and the proper subject matter of historical science. In this paper, I wish to consider Collingwood's account of historical understanding from a related but distinct perspective, that is, I wish to consider re-enactment from the perspective of Collingwood's (controversial) claim that thought stands outside time, and that it is because thought stands outside time that it is possible to understand the thoughts of other agents, including those of agents who lived in a distant past. Although Collingwood's account of re-enactment is developed in the context of his philosophy of history, it addresses some very general issues within the philosophy of mind. It is with Collingwood's approach to these general issues that the present paper is concerned: First, I explicate Collingwood's account of re-enactment against the background of his philosophy of mind and, second, attempt to show that one important goal of Collingwood's account of re-enactment is to undermine a certain type of scepticism about the possibility of sharing the thoughts of others, rather than provide a straightforward refutation of this sceptical position. While I believe Collingwood's claim that thought stands outside time to have important repercussions on how to understand [End Page 87] issues more closely related to the philosophy of history, I will not attempt, for reasons of space, to cash out these implications here. In The Idea of History2 the concept of re-enactment is expounded largely via an engagement with an imaginary objector. The position that Collingwood attributes to the imaginary objector can be preliminarily summarized as saying that the objector identifies thoughts with token acts of thinking which can be spatio-temporally located, rather than with the propositional content expressed by those token acts. It is because the objector holds thoughts to be token acts which can be spatio-temporally located that s/he denies that two agents can have or share one and the same thought. Collingwood's reply to the imaginary objector relies on a philosophy of mind that is developed elsewhere3 but whose key elements are presupposed by his discussion of re-enactment in The Idea of History, and essential to an understanding of Collingwood's position.4 An important aspect of Collingwood's philosophy of mind concerns his understanding of what is meant by an 'object of thought': When I call a thing subjective I mean that it is or pertains to a subject or conscious mind. When I call it objective I mean that it is or pertains to the object of which such a mind is conscious. I do not call a real rose objective and an imaginary one subjective, or the molecules in it objective and the beauty of it subjective. A real rose I call real and an imaginary rose I call imaginary; and I call them both objective because they are the objects of a perceiving and imagining mind respectively. (SM, 11)5 For the sake of clarity I shall refer to the subjective/objective distinction as used by Collingwood as subjective2 and objective2 and to the subjective/objective distinction against which it is contrasted as subjective1 and objective1. Objective1 means existing in...},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{robson2023acts,
  author = {Robson, Mark Ian Thomas},
  title = {Acts of Thought and Re-Enactment in {Collingwood}'s Philosophy of History},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {63},
  number = {1},
  pages = {94--111},
  doi = {10.1111/hith.12335},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Revisits Collingwood's re-enactment doctrine through the
  distinction between mental acts and their contents, arguing that the
  doctrine is more nuanced than its critics allow. Proposes that Collingwood's
  account of re-enactment as "acts of thought" provides a coherent middle
  ground between pure psychological empathy and purely logical identity of
  content.

  RELEVANCE: Updates the scholarly understanding of Collingwood's anti-speculative
  methodology for a contemporary audience, addressing objections that have
  accumulated since D'Oro's influential 2000 paper. Shows the continuing
  vitality of the Collingwoodian tradition as a hermeneutic alternative.

  POSITION: Recent Collingwood scholarship; bridges the act/content distinction
  to defend re-enactment against eliminativist objections.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Collingwood, re-enactment, philosophy-of-history, Medium},
  abstract = {This article explores one of Collingwood's most puzzling claims—that, in re‐enacting a past act of thought, I can revive not just the propositional content of that act but also the very act of thought itself. This aspect of Collingwood's ideas has been largely ignored, and, when not ignored, it has been almost universally rejected. After all, we might ask, how can it be that two acts of thought—one, say, had by Carol in the library on Wednesday and another act of thought had by Harold in his study on Thursday—are literally identical? I explore this baffling claim and, in particular, Collingwood's argument that acts of thought can have the identity of a continuant. I try to show how the idea of the identity of the continuant might be used to remove some of the puzzlement in Collingwood's claim about literal identity between acts of thought; I thus show how Harold, on Thursday, might be able to experience the exact same act of thought that Carol had on Wednesday.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{guyer2017reenactment,
  author = {Guyer, Paul},
  title = {Re-enactment, Reconstruction and the Freedom of the Imagination: {Collingwood} on History and Art},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {26},
  number = {4},
  pages = {738--758},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2017.1337559},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues for a parallel between Collingwood's re-enactment
  doctrine in the philosophy of history and his account of artistic expression
  and imagination. In both cases, the historian or artist re-creates a
  structured meaning through imaginative construction, not passive reception.
  This grounds historical understanding in a positive account of historical
  imagination rather than mere empathy.

  RELEVANCE: Enriches the anti-speculative reading of Collingwood by showing
  that re-enactment is not a mysterious faculty but involves the same
  imaginative-constructive capacities that Kant discussed in aesthetics;
  useful for the project's account of the positive hermeneutic alternative
  to speculative history.

  POSITION: Kantian-Collingwoodian synthesis; connects philosophy of history
  to aesthetics through the concept of reconstruction.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Collingwood, re-enactment, imagination, philosophy-of-history, Low},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT An implication of Kant’s aesthetics is that the audience for art must be able to meet the free play of the imagination of the artist with free play of their own imagination in order to enjoy the work of art. Does Collingwood’s conception of the aesthetic audience’s ‘reconstruction’ of the imaginative work of the artist leave room for this thought? No, but his conception of the historian’s ‘re-enactment’ of the thought of the historical subjects suggests a model for this relation that might be added to his aesthetic theory.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{fristedt2025selfknowledge,
  author = {Fristedt, Peter},
  title = {Self-Knowledge and History: {Gadamer} and {Collingwood}},
  journal = {European Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {33},
  number = {3},
  pages = {1054--1069},
  doi = {10.1111/ejop.13047},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Gadamer and Collingwood share a deep commitment to
  understanding historical knowledge as a form of self-knowledge: both hold that
  understanding the past is inseparable from understanding oneself. However,
  where Collingwood grounds this in the re-enactment of individual thought,
  Gadamer grounds it in effective-historical consciousness and the fusion of
  horizons. The paper argues for a productive synthesis that preserves
  Collingwood's focus on individual thought while incorporating Gadamer's
  insight about tradition.

  RELEVANCE: Recent scholarship that directly addresses the relationship between
  two key figures in the domain, mapping the terrain between idealist and
  ontological hermeneutics as alternatives to speculative philosophy of history.
  Shows that neither reduction to psychological empathy nor loss of the
  individual in tradition is forced by either position.

  POSITION: Comparative hermeneutics; synthesizes the British idealist and
  continental hermeneutic traditions.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Gadamer, Collingwood, hermeneutics, self-knowledge, High},
  abstract = {Quassim Cassam argues that contemporary philosophers largely neglect the kind of “substantial” self‐knowledge most people care about – knowledge of my character, beliefs, and desires – in favor of “trivial” forms of it that are nevertheless philosophically illuminating. This article takes up Cassam's challenge to turn toward accounts of substantial self‐knowledge, and, building on the work of Gadamer, makes the case that any such account has to address the question of the historical formation of the knowing subject. That historical formation – our ‘historicity’ – both erects barriers to self‐knowledge and serves as a source of it, and raises the question of how much self‐knowledge is even possible for historically situated knowers. To answer that, I take up Collingwood's claim that the aim of historical research is self‐knowledge, and his view that, since its scientific turn in the nineteenth century, history has enabled self‐knowledge of an especially significant sort. Developing these and other ideas in Collingwood, I draw a distinction between what I call “historico‐philosophical” self‐knowledge, which includes knowledge of our historicity, and “garden‐variety historical” self‐knowledge, which is focused on the particular historical formations in which we find ourselves. I argue that both count as substantial self‐knowledge and round out an understanding of myself that includes any grasp I might have of my character, beliefs, and desires.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{beiser2011weber,
  author = {Beiser, Frederick C.},
  title = {Max Weber and the End of the Historicist Tradition},
  year = {2011},
  doi = {10.1093/ACPROF:OSO/9780199691555.003.0014},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A chapter from The German Historicist Tradition examining
  how Weber's ideal-type methodology represents the terminus of the historicist
  tradition: Weber preserves the historicist emphasis on individual cultural
  values while abandoning any ambition for a unified philosophy of history.
  This amounts to a practical accommodation with the problem of historicism,
  trading speculative integration for methodological rigor.

  RELEVANCE: Shows how the historicist tradition culminates in an anti-systemic
  social science that explicitly rejects speculative philosophy of history --
  relevant to the project's account of the trajectory from Dilthey to the
  full dissolution of the speculative tradition.

  POSITION: Historical; traces the end-point of the historicist tradition
  in Weber's methodological individualism and value-pluralism.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Weber, historicism, social-science, Low},
  abstract = {Abstract The final chapter is a close examination of Weber's views on historical explanation, historical objectivity and value freedom. The first sections consider Weber's intellectual background, especially the importance of the Methodenstreit between Menger and the historical school of economics. Later sections deal with Weber's views on understanding human actions, ideal types and the limits of naturalism. The final sections examine his views on value freedom and historical objectivity.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{megill1997crisis,
  author = {Megill, Allan},
  title = {Why Was There a Crisis of Historicism?},
  journal = {History and Theory},
  year = {1997},
  volume = {36},
  number = {3},
  pages = {416--429},
  doi = {10.1111/0018-2656.00024},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that the "crisis of historicism" (c. 1900--1930) was
  not primarily a logical crisis (the self-refutation of relativism) but a
  practical-cultural crisis: the collapse of belief in historical progress
  and the unity of Western civilization that made any universal scheme of
  history seem credible. The crisis was precipitated by World War I, not by
  philosophical argumentation.

  RELEVANCE: Provides crucial contextual argument for understanding why the
  hermeneutic critique of speculative history became so pressing in the early
  twentieth century. Shows that the appeal to historicist particularism against
  Hegelian universalism was motivated by real historical disruption, not mere
  philosophical scruple.

  POSITION: Historical-contextual; sociological explanation of the historicist
  crisis rather than internal philosophical reconstruction.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, historicism, relativism, crisis, philosophy-of-history, Medium},
  abstract = {Book reviewed in this article: Heidegger, Dilthey, and the Crisis of Historicism By Charles R. Bambach},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{kinzel2016windelband,
  author = {Kinzel, Katherina},
  title = {Wilhelm {Windelband} and the Problem of Relativism},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2016},
  volume = {25},
  number = {1},
  pages = {84--107},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2016.1228602},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines how Windelband, the Neo-Kantian founder of the
  ideographic/nomothetic distinction, attempted to respond to the relativism
  that his own historicist commitments seemed to generate. Windelband sought
  to ground historical values in a transcendental notion of normative validity
  that would be independent of historical contingency -- a position Kinzel
  argues is ultimately unstable given his own historicist premises.

  RELEVANCE: Traces the Neo-Kantian background to the hermeneutic tradition's
  problem with relativism, showing that the standard response (transcendental
  grounding of values) was already recognized as insufficient by Windelband's
  own successors. Relevant to the project's question about whether historicism
  refutes or merely relativizes speculative universalism.

  POSITION: Neo-Kantian historicism; maps the tension between the ideographic
  method and the threat of value-relativism.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Windelband, Neo-Kantianism, relativism, historicism, Medium},
  abstract = {This paper analyzes the shifts in Wilhelm Windelband’s ‘critical philosophy of values’ as it developed hand in hand with his understanding of relativism. The paper has two goals. On the one hand, by analyzing the role that relativism played in his philosophical project, it seeks to contribute to a better understanding of Windelband's intellectual development in the context of historicism and Neo-Kantianism. On the other hand, by highlighting Windelband’s contribution to the understanding of relativism, it sheds light on an important episode in the history of that problem. The paper distinguishes between three phases in Windelband’s thinking and shows that his views about relativism changed in close connection with his conception of history. The early Windelband thought that historicism was compatible with absolute validity because he was firmly convinced of historical progress. The mature Windelband rejected progress as a means for justifying validity and put the problem of relativism into sharp relief. In response to the failure of his mature philosophy to fend off relativism, the later Windelband strengthened the role of history again. The paper concludes that Windelband’s significance lies not in his arguments against relativism, but rather in having furthered philosophical understanding of the problems at stake.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{woodford2012specters,
  author = {Woodford, Peter},
  title = {Specters of the Nineteenth Century: {Charles Taylor} and the Problem of Historicism},
  journal = {Journal of Religious Ethics},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {40},
  number = {1},
  pages = {171--192},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1467-9795.2011.00514.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines how Charles Taylor negotiates the problem of
  historicism in Sources of the Self and other works: Taylor seeks to defend
  the significance of "strong evaluation" (irreducibly qualitative moral
  distinctions) while acknowledging that our frameworks for such evaluation
  are historically situated. Argues that Taylor inherits the nineteenth-century
  historicist dilemma and his solution -- ontological hermeneutics -- is
  structurally similar to Gadamer's.

  RELEVANCE: Shows how the problem of historicism -- can historically situated
  understanding support normative claims? -- persists into contemporary
  analytic-hermeneutic philosophy, with implications for the research project's
  question about whether the hermeneutic alternative to speculative history
  can sustain any universalist commitments.

  POSITION: Contemporary hermeneutic ethics; the problem of historicism
  re-posed within analytic philosophy through Taylor.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Taylor, historicism, ethics, relativism, Low},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT This paper identifies and analyzes the problem of historicism in Charles Taylor's work overall, but with particular emphasis on his most recent publication, A Secular Age. I circumscribe the problem of historicism through reference to the nineteenth‐century German philosophical tradition in which it developed, in particular in the thought of Wilhelm Dilthey. I then trace the structural similarities between the notions of history to be found in the thought of Taylor and Dilthey and how these structural similarities raise worries associated with the problem of historicism. I argue that the structural aporia of historicism evident in Taylor's work brings to light a live philosophical problem that is basic to theoretical debates in the study religion.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{mouzakitis2014narrative,
  author = {Mouzakitis, Angelos},
  title = {From Narrative to Action: {Paul Ricoeur}'s Reflections on History},
  journal = {Rethinking History},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {19},
  number = {3},
  pages = {393--408},
  doi = {10.1080/13642529.2014.901654},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Ricoeur's hermeneutics of history -- developed
  across Time and Narrative and Memory, History, Forgetting -- provides a
  "third way" between positivist explanation and speculative-teleological
  narration. Narrative configuration (mimesis) gives historical time a
  coherence that resists reduction to mere chronicle, but without imposing
  a trans-historical Geist or teleological necessity. The concept of the
  "quasi-plot" of history respects contingency while providing meaning.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the research project's question about the
  hermeneutic alternative to speculative history: Ricoeur's narrative
  hermeneutics is precisely an attempt to preserve historical meaning without
  Hegelian speculation, making it a crucial test case for whether the
  hermeneutic critique leaves room for any totalizing historical thought.

  POSITION: Ricoeur hermeneutics; the narrative alternative to speculative
  philosophy of history as coherent but anti-teleological synthesis.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Ricoeur, narrative, history, hermeneutics, High},
  abstract = {This paper attempts to engage critically with Ricoeur's multifaceted and multilayered approach to history. Drawing on a vast number of philosophical, historical and sociological works, Ricoeur's hermeneutics develops as an invaluable corpus of writings on virtually all the aspects of history and historicity alike. This paper focuses primarily on Ricoeur's attempt to enrich further the interpretation of human historicity provided by the phenomenological-hermeneutic tradition and his consequent attempt to identify and indicate the liberating potential inherent in human action. Specific emphasis is placed in the role of body and of habitus in the very shaping of socio-historical worlds, while the place of birth (natality), death (finitude) and murder is also considered both in regard of their factual and ethical implications for human life. Correlative are the problems of self-identity, subjective and collective memory, subjective temporality and co-temporality, which are also in the centre of Ricoeur's meditations on history and thus of great importance for the purposes of the present work. At the same time, this paper touches on epistemological issues, as it examines the intricate ties that bind together understanding and explanation, structure and event, fiction and historical narrative, reality and imagination, history and truth. Finally, history is always addressed from the perspective of action as responsibility and utopian promise.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@misc{patton2022dilthey,
  author = {Patton, Lydia},
  title = {Understanding, Psychology, and the Human Sciences: {Dilthey} and {V{\"o}lkerpsychologie}},
  year = {2022},
  howpublished = {In \emph{The History of Understanding in Analytic Philosophy}, Bloomsbury Publishing},
  doi = {10.5040/9781350159235.ch-002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Traces how Dilthey's conception of Verstehen was shaped by
  and distinguished from the Volkerpsychologie program (Lazarus, Steinthal).
  Argues that Dilthey's mature hermeneutics represents a decisive break from
  the attempt to assimilate the human sciences to a naturalistic descriptive
  psychology: understanding in Dilthey is ultimately a logical-structural
  achievement, not a psychological one.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the analytic history-of-philosophy context for
  understanding why Dilthey's Verstehen doctrine diverges from empirical
  psychology and retains systematic philosophical significance. Situates
  Dilthey's critique of positivist history within the broader debate about
  naturalism in the human sciences.

  POSITION: Analytic history of philosophy of science; historical contextualization
  of the Diltheyan Verstehen doctrine.
  },
  keywords = {hermeneutics-historicism, Dilthey, Verstehen, psychology, human-sciences, Medium},
  abstract = {At the end of the eighteenth century, in the Metaphysical Foundations of Natural Science , Kant argued that the only “true” sciences were those that could be given a mathematical foundation. He exempted psychology from the true sciences, arguing that no mathematical analysis could be given of the occurrence of thoughts and ideas. Johann Friedrich Herbart and others in the tradition of empirical psychology (later including Wundt, Fechner, and Weber) took Kant’s words as a challenge, coming up with ways to quantify sensation and perception and to test them experimentally. But Kant’s more fundamental distinction between mathematically based, a priori sciences and inductive, a posteriori sciences based on observation lingered. The schools of neo-Kantianism that followed took differing positions on Kant’s stance. The Southwest School, or Baden School, argued for a dualism between nature and value, taking the position that beyond the objects present to us, there are also meaningful and valid relations of valuation. The realm of value can be treated scientifi cally as well ( Š uber 2009 : § 14.2.2). CHAPTER THREE},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{chakrabarty2000provincializing,
  author = {Chakrabarty, Dipesh},
  title = {Provincializing Europe: Postcolonial Thought and Historical Difference},
  year = {2000},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  address = {Princeton, NJ},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Chakrabarty argues that European historical thought -- including
  Hegel's stadial historicism -- functions as a master narrative that assigns
  non-European histories the role of catching up: they are always "not yet"
  what Europe already is. This "historicism" is not merely parochial but
  structurally necessary to capital and modernity. Chakrabarty proposes
  provincializing Europe by showing how subaltern histories exceed and
  destabilize the categories (labor, capital, citizenship) through which
  historicism frames them; he develops the idea of "History 1" (universal
  abstract history of capital) vs. "History 2" (concrete life-practices
  that are not subsumed).

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the speculative tradition's universalizing
  temporal structure; Hegel's "world history" is Chakrabarty's paradigm case
  of historicism. The "first in Europe, then elsewhere" structure is identified
  as the key mechanism by which speculative history marginalizes non-Western
  peoples, and its critique is the central contribution of postcolonial theory
  to philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Postcolonial provincializing -- retains some universals while
  showing their provincial origins and limitations.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, historicism, Eurocentrism, subaltern, Hegel, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{fanon1961wretched,
  author = {Fanon, Frantz},
  title = {The Wretched of the Earth},
  year = {1961},
  publisher = {Grove Press},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fanon analyzes colonialism as a Manichaean world divided into
  the colonizer's zone and the colonized's zone, arguing that decolonization
  cannot be achieved through adoption of European liberalism but requires a
  rupture with European modernity's self-understanding. The colonial subject
  is not the Hegelian bondsman who achieves self-consciousness through labor;
  they are excluded from dialectical recognition entirely, rendered a thing.
  Fanon's dialectic of violence challenges the idealist notion that history
  progresses through rational mediation.

  RELEVANCE: Constitutes the foundational challenge to Hegelian historical
  teleology from the colonial subject's perspective. Fanon shows that the
  colonial world is structured by the negation of dialectical development for
  the colonized; speculative history's promised telos of freedom is experienced
  as its inverse. Essential for understanding what any post-Hegelian philosophy
  of history must account for.

  POSITION: Anticolonial existential-phenomenological critique of speculative
  historical teleology.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Fanon, dialectic, colonial-history, violence, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{fanon1952blackskin,
  author = {Fanon, Frantz},
  title = {Black Skin, White Masks},
  year = {1952},
  publisher = {Grove Press},
  address = {New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fanon applies existential phenomenology and psychoanalysis to
  analyze the colonized subject's double consciousness -- being interpellated
  as inferior within a world structured by white norms. The "Lived Experience
  of the Black Man" chapter demonstrates that the Hegelian struggle for
  recognition is unavailable to the colonized Black subject: recognition is
  denied at the level of ontology, not just social position, because the
  colonial racial schema has already foreclosed reciprocal recognition.

  RELEVANCE: Identifies the racial limit of Hegelian recognition theory: the
  master-slave dialectic presupposes two parties capable of mutual recognition,
  but colonialism structures one party as below the threshold of recognition.
  This is one of the earliest philosophical critiques of speculative history's
  exclusions from the perspective of those excluded.

  POSITION: Existential-phenomenological critique of the racial structure of
  Hegelian recognition and dialectical history.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Fanon, recognition, race, dialectic, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{spivak1999critique,
  author = {Spivak, Gayatri Chakravorty},
  title = {A Critique of Postcolonial Reason: Toward a History of the Vanishing Present},
  year = {1999},
  publisher = {Harvard University Press},
  address = {Cambridge, MA},
  doi = {10.2307/j.ctvjsf541},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Spivak tracks the figure of the "native informant" -- the
  foreclosed voice that enables the production of philosophical and historical
  knowledge -- across Kant, Hegel, Marx, and colonial literature. In the Hegel
  chapter she shows how the native informant is the constitutive outside of
  speculative reason: the absolute must exclude precisely those who cannot be
  integrated into the dialectical narrative. This extends and complicates her
  earlier essay "Can the Subaltern Speak?" (1988), which argued that the
  subaltern woman is systematically doubly excluded from both colonial and
  nationalist historical narratives.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most sustained philosophical treatment of how
  speculative philosophy (Kant and Hegel especially) produces its universal
  claims by marking off a constitutive outside that cannot speak within those
  categories. Directly relevant to the question of whether subaltern
  experiences can be represented within or must rupture speculative history.

  POSITION: Postcolonial feminist deconstruction of speculative reason's
  universalist self-presentation.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Spivak, subaltern, Hegel, representation, feminist, High}
}

@article{mbembe2003necropolitics,
  author = {Mbembe, Achille},
  title = {Necropolitics},
  journal = {Public Culture},
  year = {2003},
  volume = {15},
  number = {1},
  pages = {11--40},
  doi = {10.1215/08992363-15-1-11},
  abstract = {In this essay Cameroonian researcher Achille Mbembe draws on Michel Foucault's concept of sovereign power, Giorgio Agamben's discourse on homo sacer, and Frantz Fanon's decolonial approach to situate the notion of biopower in the space of postcolony and demonstrates its insufficiency for understanding contemporary forms of subjugating life to the power of death. Mbembe introduces the notion of necropolitics, or necropower, to explain the various ways in which weapons are used in the modern world to create 'worlds of death'. He argues that the colony in general and slave plantations in particular gave rise to those necropolitical practices that remain relevant today. Mbembe describes the postcolonial locus as a place where diffuse and not always exclusively state power inscribes an 'economy of death' into relations of governance and production, and argues that whereas in the colonial era violence was solely a means of achieving profit, in the space of postcolony it becomes an end in itself.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Drawing on Foucault's biopower and Agamben's homo sacer,
  Mbembe argues that the colonial and plantation contexts gave rise to forms
  of sovereignty whose primary aim is not the administration of life but the
  dispensation of death -- "necropower." The colony is the space in which
  the exception is normalized, sovereignty exercised as the right to kill, and
  the slave plantation as a space of living death. This framework challenges
  Hegelian and Enlightenment narratives of history as progressive unfolding
  of freedom by showing that modernity has always been organized around death
  for colonized subjects.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most influential framework for understanding colonial
  temporality as constitutively different from -- and not merely lagging behind
  -- the speculative history of freedom. Shows that the "world history" of
  speculative philosophy is premised on the necropolitical management of those
  assigned to the colonial exception.

  POSITION: Postcolonial critical theory -- modernity's underside as
  constitutive rather than marginal or accidental.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Mbembe, necropolitics, sovereignty, colonial-history, High}
}

@book{mignolo2000local,
  author = {Mignolo, Walter D.},
  title = {Local Histories/Global Designs: Coloniality, Subaltern Knowledges, and Border Thinking},
  year = {2000},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  address = {Princeton, NJ},
  doi = {10.23943/princeton/9780691156095.001.0001},
  abstract = {This book is an extended argument about the "coloniality" of power. In a shrinking world where sharp dichotomies, such as East/West and developing/developed, blur and shift, this book points to the inadequacy of current practices in the social sciences and area studies. It explores the crucial notion of "colonial difference" in the study of the modern colonial world and traces the emergence of an epistemic shift, which the book calls "border thinking." Further, the book expands the horizons of those debates already under way in postcolonial studies of Asia and Africa by dwelling on the genealogy of thoughts of South/Central America, the Caribbean, and Latino/as in the United States. The book's concept of "border gnosis," or sensing and knowing by dwelling in imperial/colonial borderlands, counters the tendency of occidentalist perspectives to manage, and thus limit, understanding. A new preface discusses this book as a dialogue with Hegel's Philosophy of History.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mignolo argues that modernity and coloniality are inseparable:
  the production of global designs (the universal) is always enabled by local
  histories of colonial violence. "Border thinking" -- thinking from the
  subaltern colonial difference -- offers an alternative to both Eurocentric
  universalism and nativist particularism. Against Hegel's world history,
  Mignolo proposes a decolonial epistemology that foregrounds the colonial
  wound as the starting point for a different understanding of modernity,
  temporality, and knowledge.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most systematic decolonial alternative to the
  Hegelian world-historical schema. Where Chakrabarty provincializes from
  within postcolonial theory, Mignolo proposes "decolonial options" that
  begin from outside European epistemic categories entirely. Cross-domain
  connection to Domain 4 (Frankfurt School and critical theory) via Mignolo's
  explicit critique of Habermasian communicative reason as itself Eurocentric.

  POSITION: Decolonial option -- modernity/coloniality are inseparable;
  border thinking as epistemic alternative.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Mignolo, decolonial, coloniality, border-thinking, High}
}

@book{mignolo2011darker,
  author = {Mignolo, Walter D.},
  title = {The Darker Side of Western Modernity: Global Futures, Decolonial Options},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Duke University Press},
  address = {Durham, NC},
  doi = {10.1215/9780822394501},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mignolo extends his decolonial project by systematically
  contrasting the "rhetoric of modernity" (salvation, progress, development)
  with the "logic of coloniality" that makes it possible. The decolonial
  option is not an alternative modernity but a delinking from the
  modernity/coloniality matrix. Hegel's philosophy of history is analyzed
  as the philosophical apex of the rhetoric of modernity: the teleological
  narrative that positions Europe as the telos of world history.

  RELEVANCE: Offers the most developed account of how speculative philosophy
  of history functions ideologically, and what a decolonial alternative would
  look like. Directly responds to the question: what do decolonial thinkers
  offer in place of Hegelian universal history?

  POSITION: Mature decolonial alternative -- delinking from the speculative
  tradition rather than reforming it from within.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Mignolo, decolonial, coloniality, modernity, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{stone2017hegel,
  author = {Stone, Alison},
  title = {Hegel and Colonialism},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {41},
  number = {2},
  pages = {247--270},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2017.17},
  abstract = {Abstract This article explores the implications of Hegel's Philosophy of World History with respect to colonialism. For Hegel, freedom can be recognized and practised only in classical, Christian and modern Europe; therefore, the world's other peoples can acquire freedom only if Europeans impose their civilization upon them. Although this imposition denies freedom to colonized peoples, this denial is legitimate for Hegel because it is the sole condition on which these peoples can gain freedom in the longer term. The article then considers whether Hegel's basic account of freedom can be extricated from his Eurocentric and pro-colonialist interpretation of the course of history. The article argues that matters are more complicated because that interpretation has significant connections with Hegel's conception of freedom as self-determination.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Stone argues that Hegel's idea of freedom as liberation from
  nature is internally connected to his negative characterization of non-European
  peoples as immersed in nature and therefore unfree. This means that the
  systematic concept of freedom -- not just Hegel's empirical racial
  pronouncements -- presupposes a colonial logic in which European freedom
  is defined against the non-European other. Stone concludes that this
  contamination cannot be removed by simple revision; one cannot "save Hegel
  from himself" by excising the colonial passages.

  RELEVANCE: Advances the racial structure thesis to the level of the concept
  of freedom itself: the core normative concept of speculative philosophy of
  history is shown to be internally Eurocentric. This is the strongest form
  of the objection, since it targets the principle rather than the application.

  POSITION: Racial structure thesis -- freedom as a concept is constitutively
  colonial in Hegel's philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Stone, Hegel, colonialism, freedom, High}
}

@book{james2025hegel,
  author = {James, Daniel and Knappik, Franz},
  title = {Hegel and Colonialism},
  year = {2025},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  doi = {10.1017/9781009587143},
  abstract = {This Element offers the first comprehensive study of Hegel's views on European colonialism. It shows that Hegel's accounts of issues like freedom, personhood and the dialectic of lordship and bondage are deeply entangled with his disturbing views on colonialism, slavery, and race. This title is also available as open access on Cambridge Core.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The first comprehensive study of Hegel's views on European
  colonialism, reconstructing his scattered discussions as forming a systematic
  account: colonialism as racial domination underpinned by central elements of
  his philosophy. James and Knappik show that Hegel's discussions of freedom,
  personhood, and the lord-bondsman dialectic are deeply entangled with his
  views on race, slavery, and the justifiability of colonial domination. They
  situate Hegel in overlooked contexts including British abolitionism and
  Scottish four-stages theories.

  RELEVANCE: The most comprehensive recent scholarly treatment of the specific
  textual and systematic connections between Hegel's speculative philosophy and
  his colonial apologetics. Provides the authoritative scholarly mapping of
  the problem that other contributions in this domain respond to.

  POSITION: Racial structure thesis -- comprehensive scholarly documentation
  of Hegel's pro-colonial arguments and their systematic roots.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, James, Knappik, Hegel, colonialism, race, High}
}

@article{jaarte2024colonial,
  author = {Jaarte, Miikka},
  title = {Colonial Slavery, the Lord-Bondsman Dialectic, and the {St Louis Hegelians}},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {45},
  number = {1},
  pages = {43--64},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2024.3},
  abstract = {Abstract Hegel's lord-bondsman dialectic has been of especially great interest to progressive and radical Hegelians -- broadly speaking, politically left-leaning interpreters of Hegel who object to certain social hierarchies and demand their abolition. They read Hegel as giving an account of how 'lordship' over others is an inherently unstable and unsatisfying social formation, even for its supposed beneficiaries. Marxists, feminists and post-colonial theorists have all found inspiration in Hegel's analysis of the lord and bondsman by applying it to concrete relations of oppression, such as capitalism, patriarchy, or racism. In contrast, recent scholarship on Hegel's views on race and colonialism has cast doubt on whether his systematic political philosophy and philosophy of history can be of similar use to progressives. Hegel was undeniably a Eurocentric thinker, but philosophers like Robert Bernasconi and Alison Stone have convincingly shown that he was also a racist and a defender of colonialism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Jaarte argues that Hegel's lord-bondsman dialectic cannot be
  separated from his pro-colonialism: Hegel himself used the dialectic to argue
  that colonial slavery educates its victims and is therefore temporarily
  justifiable. This reading was recognized and embraced by the St Louis
  Hegelians, who used it to defend pre-Civil War American slavery -- showing
  that the progressive use of the dialectic (for emancipation) was not the
  only available reading, and that the colonial-apologetic reading is equally
  internal to the text.

  RELEVANCE: Shows that the progressive reappropriation of the lord-bondsman
  dialectic (by Marxists, feminists, and postcolonial theorists) must contend
  with Hegel's own use of it to justify colonial domination. This deepens the
  critique of speculative history by showing its ambiguity is not an accident
  of reception history but is rooted in the original text.

  POSITION: Racial structure thesis -- the dialectic itself is available for
  colonial-apologetic and emancipatory readings alike, which undermines naive
  progressive uses.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Jaarte, Hegel, lord-bondsman-dialectic, slavery, colonialism, Medium}
}

@article{murthy2024rescuing,
  author = {Murthy, Viren},
  title = {Rescuing {Hegel} from {Eurocentrism}: {Oriental} Reconstructions of {Hegel}'s {Orient}},
  journal = {Hegel Bulletin},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {45},
  number = {2},
  pages = {368--395},
  doi = {10.1017/hgl.2024.13},
  abstract = {Abstract Hegel's Eurocentrism has become something of an elephant in the room, too obvious to merit discussion. The secondary literature on Hegel often concedes that many of Hegel's comments about the Orient are misguided and uninformed, and then declares that such remarks are of no consequence to his philosophy. However, recently, some scholars have contended that Hegel's view of freedom is fundamentally connected to his negative vision of the Orient and Africa. For example, Alison Stone asserts that Hegel's idea of freedom is premised on his Eurocentrism and colonialism. If Stone's contention is true, there would be no way to, as she puts it, save Hegel from himself. I argue that we can move some way towards rescuing Hegel by noting how scholars from modern Asia incorporated some of Hegel's ideas to struggle against Eurocentrism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Against Stone's claim that Hegel's concept of freedom is
  constitutively Eurocentric and cannot be saved, Murthy argues that Asian
  scholars (specifically Okakura Tenshin in Meiji Japan) used Hegel's ideas
  to resist Western cultural imperialism. Hegel's critique of abstract
  individualist freedom, when applied by non-Western thinkers, can be turned
  against Eurocentrism itself. This represents a partial rehabilitation of
  the speculative tradition: not all of Hegel is contaminated by his colonial
  passages.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a counterpoint to the dominant critical literature and
  raises the question of whether the speculative tradition can be appropriated
  against its own Eurocentric uses -- a question directly pertinent to any
  synthesis of this domain.

  POSITION: Partial rehabilitation -- Hegel's universalism can be decolonized
  through non-Western reception and creative reappropriation.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Murthy, Hegel, Eurocentrism, rehabilitation, Medium}
}

@article{timmenga2025critique,
  author = {Timmenga, Friso and Van der Zande, Indira and Zwitter, Andrej},
  title = {Critique and Alterity in {Enrique Dussel}'s Decolonial Philosophy},
  journal = {Philosophy},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {100},
  number = {2},
  pages = {221--247},
  doi = {10.1017/S0031819125000087},
  abstract = {Abstract This paper analyses the tension between the double focus on critique and alterity within decolonial discourses. We argue that an excess of critical thinking could lead to scepticism, whereas an overemphasis on alterity could result in dogmatism. Consequently, since both approaches end up obstructing epistemic decolonization, we argue that it is necessary to strike a balance between critique and alterity; a balance that does not resolve the tension, but seeks to reveal its underlying relation. The first section locates decolonial theory within the framework of critical theory. We highlight how Quijano, Dussel and Mignolo invoke the critical tradition, whilst simultaneously claiming that a radical departure from it is necessary. Turning more explicitly to Dussel in Section 2, we explain Dussel's analectics as a method to localize discourses, in which globally excluded perspectives are foregrounded. In the third section, we defend Dussel's universalism and rationalism against criticisms from Castro-Gomez and Vallega by interpreting him as a relational thinker. Ultimately, the paper concludes that the success of epistemic decolonization hinges on its ability to reconcile classic notions of universality and rationality in a manner that avoids dogmatism and scepticism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The paper analyzes a central tension in decolonial theory
  between critique (which risks scepticism) and alterity (which risks
  dogmatism). Drawing on Dussel's analectics as a method of localizing
  discourse by foregrounding globally excluded perspectives, the authors
  defend Dussel's universalism and rationalism against critics, arguing
  he is a relational thinker who reconciles universality with material
  grounding in colonial exteriority. Decolonial philosophy requires both
  critical distance from European rationality and affirmative recovery of
  suppressed traditions.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most recent analytical engagement with Dussel's
  alternative to Hegelian universal history, assessing whether his
  "philosophy of liberation" offers a coherent non-Eurocentric alternative
  that avoids the twin pitfalls of scepticism and relativism -- a question
  directly pertinent to any assessment of decolonial alternatives to
  speculative history.

  POSITION: Decolonial alternative -- Dussel's analectics as constructive
  response to speculative universal history.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Dussel, Mignolo, decolonial, universalism, Medium}
}

@article{vazquez2018critical,
  author = {V{\'a}zquez-Arroyo, Antonio Y.},
  title = {Critical Theory, Colonialism, and the Historicity of Thought},
  journal = {Constellations},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {25},
  number = {1},
  pages = {54--70},
  doi = {10.1111/1467-8675.12348},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Vazquez-Arroyo critiques critical theory (Frankfurt School and
  its contemporary heirs) for retaining a Eurocentric historicism even while
  claiming to overcome it. The "historicity of thought" -- the claim that
  all thought is historically conditioned -- is itself deployed in a way that
  privileges European historical experience as the site of genuinely critical
  reflection. Postcolonial critiques of critical theory's Eurocentric universalism
  are surveyed and the prospects for a genuinely decolonized critical theory
  assessed sceptically.

  RELEVANCE: Connects the postcolonial critique of Hegelian historicism to its
  Frankfurt School reception, providing the critical theory perspective on the
  domain-bridging question: does the critique of speculative history extend to
  its Hegelian-Marxist heirs?

  POSITION: Sceptical decolonial critique of critical theory's Eurocentric
  residue.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, critical-theory, historicism, Eurocentrism, domain-4-crossref, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{chanter2010antigone,
  author = {Chanter, Tina},
  title = {Antigone's Liminality: {Hegel}'s Racial Purification of Tragedy and the Naturalization of Slavery},
  booktitle = {Hegel's Philosophy and Feminist Thought},
  year = {2010},
  pages = {61--85},
  publisher = {Palgrave Macmillan US},
  doi = {10.1057/9780230110410_4},
  abstract = {The Sophoclean tragic cycle stands exemplary of Western culture in so many diverse ways, the exemplarity of which has been expounded by various philosophical, psychoanalytic, and literary figures, some of whom -- G. W. F. Hegel among them -- have themselves founded schools of thought. Yet all too rarely have the exponents of Sophocles' Oedipus or Antigone been willing or able to take on and think through the paradox that these literary, philosophical, psychoanalytic heroes were penned by an aristocratic author whose "leisure" time to conceive, write, and perform his exemplary tragedies was bought at the expense of a system of chattel slavery. The achievements of ancient Athenian society are glorified in a manner that encourages a certain evasion of our own implication in empires built on slavery and colonialism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Chanter argues that Hegel's interpretation of Antigone --
  as the representative of natural ethical life and family piety, excluded
  from civil society and the state -- racially purifies the tragedy by
  ignoring the play's engagement with slavery and foreignness. The figure
  of Antigone in Hegel's Phenomenology naturalizes both the exclusion of
  women from public life and the colonial naturalization of slavery: both
  are presented as expressions of immediacy rather than freedom. The feminist
  and postcolonial critiques of Hegel converge in the figure of Antigone.

  RELEVANCE: Constitutes the key text at the intersection of feminist and
  postcolonial critiques of speculative history: shows how gender exclusion
  and racial exclusion are structurally connected in Hegel's treatment of
  Antigone. Directly relevant to questions about Hegel's gendered dialectic
  and its racial dimensions.

  POSITION: Feminist-postcolonial convergence -- the exclusion of women and
  the exclusion of the colonized are systematically connected in the speculative
  framework.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Chanter, Hegel, Antigone, feminist, race, gender, Medium}
}

@book{mills1996feminist,
  author = {Mills, Patricia Jagentowicz},
  title = {Feminist Interpretations of {G. W. F. Hegel}},
  year = {1996},
  publisher = {Pennsylvania State University Press},
  address = {University Park, PA},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A collection of essays bringing together the major feminist
  philosophical engagements with Hegel's treatment of women -- his account
  of women as belonging to natural ethical life (the family, Antigone),
  his characterization of women as "the irony of the community," and his
  exclusion of women from civil society, the state, and the full exercise
  of Sittlichkeit. The collection contains contributions from thinkers
  including Seyla Benhabib, Drucilla Cornell, and others, representing
  multiple feminist approaches from liberal to psychoanalytic to Butlerian.

  RELEVANCE: The foundational reference for feminist philosophical critique
  of Hegel's speculative treatment of gender. Essential for understanding
  how Hegel's philosophy of history marginalizes women by assigning them to
  the "pre-historical" domestic sphere in a way structurally analogous to
  his treatment of Africa and Asia as prehistorical.

  POSITION: Feminist critique -- women are systematically excluded from or
  subordinated within the dialectic of speculative history.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Mills, Hegel, feminist, gender, Antigone, women, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@misc{spivak1988subaltern,
  author = {Spivak, Gayatri Chakravorty},
  title = {Can the Subaltern Speak?},
  year = {1988},
  howpublished = {In Cary Nelson and Lawrence Grossberg (eds.), \emph{Marxism and the Interpretation of Culture}. University of Illinois Press},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Spivak argues that the academic recovery of subaltern voices
  -- particularly by post-structuralist thinkers like Deleuze and Foucault,
  and by subaltern studies historians -- systematically re-covers the very
  voices it claims to uncover, because representation (both in the sense of
  political speaking-for and aesthetic depiction) requires codes and frameworks
  that were generated within and exclude the subaltern's perspective. The
  figure of the Hindu widow (sati) illustrates double exclusion: between
  patriarchal tradition and colonial legislation, the subaltern woman has no
  space to speak. The subaltern cannot speak within existing historical
  frameworks -- including the speculative one.

  RELEVANCE: Foundational text for the postcolonial critique of historical
  representation: any philosophy of history that claims to speak for or
  about subaltern peoples re-enacts the violence of exclusion. Directly
  relevant to the question of whether speculative history can incorporate
  non-Western and non-elite subjects.

  POSITION: Postcolonial feminist critique of representation -- the structural
  conditions of speculative history exclude subaltern speech.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Spivak, subaltern, representation, feminist, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{tibebu2011hegel,
  author = {Tibebu, Teshale},
  title = {Hegel and the Third World: The Making of Eurocentrism in World History},
  year = {2011},
  publisher = {Syracuse University Press},
  address = {Syracuse, NY},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tibebu provides a sustained examination of how Hegel's
  philosophy of history produces a Eurocentric conception of world history
  by systematically assigning Africa, Asia, and the Americas to stages of
  history that are presented as completed by European spirit. The categories
  of race, gender, and class are shown to intersect in Hegel's construction
  of world history, with Africa positioned as "the domain of the senses"
  outside historical time. The book traces how Hegelian categories shaped
  subsequent academic historiography in ways that continue to underwrite
  Eurocentrism.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a global intellectual history perspective on Hegel's
  role in producing the Eurocentrism that postcolonial critiques target. Useful
  for establishing the reception history of speculative history and its
  ideological effects.

  POSITION: Postcolonial critique -- Hegel as the intellectual architect of
  Eurocentrism in academic historiography.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Tibebu, Hegel, Eurocentrism, Africa, world-history, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@misc{mbembe2001postcolony,
  author = {Mbembe, Achille},
  title = {On the Postcolony},
  year = {2001},
  howpublished = {University of California Press, Berkeley},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mbembe analyzes the postcolony as a specific political
  formation characterized by the entanglement of power, violence, and excess,
  irreducible to either the colonial or the properly postcolonial. Against
  both Hegelian narratives of historical progress and their liberal variants,
  Mbembe shows that postcolonial African societies are structured by a "time
  of entanglement" -- not teleological but multiple, fractured, and
  heterogeneous. The commandement (colonial-postcolonial state power) operates
  through grotesque excess rather than rational governance.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a theory of postcolonial temporality as constitutively
  non-teleological, directly challenging the Hegelian philosophy of history's
  claim to universal temporal structure. Complements the Necropolitics essay
  with a fuller treatment of the African political context.

  POSITION: Postcolonial critique -- postcolonial time is heterogeneous and
  fractured, not a phase within speculative historical teleology.
  },
  keywords = {postcolonial-decolonial-feminist, Mbembe, postcolony, temporality, Africa, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{popper1945open,
  author    = {Popper, Karl R.},
  title     = {The Open Society and Its Enemies},
  year      = {1945},
  publisher = {Routledge and Kegan Paul},
  address   = {London},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Popper traces the intellectual ancestry of totalitarianism
  to philosophical "holists" and "historicists"---above all Plato, Hegel, and
  Marx---who share the view that individuals are subordinated to the logic of
  historical or organic social wholes. Volume I ("The Spell of Plato") attacks
  Platonic political philosophy as proto-totalitarian; Volume II ("The High Tide
  of Prophecy") diagnoses Hegel's and Marx's historical determinism as the
  theoretical engine of twentieth-century collectivism. The book articulates a
  liberal "open society" as the political counterpart to falsificationist
  epistemology.

  RELEVANCE: This work connects Popper's epistemological anti-historicism
  directly to political theory and to the history of speculative philosophy.
  The sustained critiques of Hegel and Marx---as the key representatives of
  the speculative tradition---are directly relevant to a literature review on
  that tradition. Cross-domain note: Domain 6 (political philosophy) and the
  present domain overlap here.

  POSITION: Second founding statement of Popperian anti-historicism, now with
  explicit political stakes and historical-philosophical targets.
  },
  keywords = {anti-historicism-philosophy-social-science, Popper, open-society, abstract = {Roslyn Weiss'sThe Socratic Paradox and Its Enemiespresents a novel and ambitious interpretation of the familiar Socratic paradoxes: that virtue is knowledge, that all the virtues are one, and that no one does wrong willingly. According to Weiss, the common interpretation of these paradoxes are not really "Socratic" in the sense that Socrates himself holds those views. Socrates does not intellectualize virtue; what he means by the claim that virtue is knowledge is only that virtuous people choose virtue wisely. Socrates does not believe that the parts of virtue such as justice, temperance, courage, etc. are manifestations of a single moral craft; he only believes that these virtues are all required for a life rightly lived. And most importantly, Socrates does not make the implausible claim that no one who knows what is right does wrong and thus every wrongdoing involves some kind of error.He rather maintains that bad people do wrong intentionally either because they are overcome by their passions or appetites or because they think injustice is to their advantage, and that these bad people are blameworthy because they can (and should) overcome their desires and fears, and they can (and should) act against what they believe is to their advantage in case doing that involves doing bad things.
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

So, if Weiss is right, the views Socrates actually holds are not really paradoxical at all. It is true that they are often presented in rather paradoxical forms. But that is only because, Weiss claims, Socrates is reacting rather vigorously against the views held by his opponents, the "enemies" of justice. Weiss draws the reader's attention to various passages in Platonic dialogues where war-imagery is used, and suggests that Socrates is a fighter in a war for justice. Various dialogues are battlefields, so to speak, and Socrates takes on a different tactic in accordance with the opponent in each battle. In order to win the battle, Socrates sometimes overstates his case, sometimes arguing for conclusions that he regards as false, and sometimes even intentionally misleading his opponent in order to trap him. As a result, Weiss contends, "not every Socratic utterance is a Socratic view, and not every Socratic utterance that is a Socratic view means what it seems to mean" (p.8).

In order to understand and appreciate what Socrates really means by his paradox-like statements, then, one would have to pay close attention to the contexts in which Socrates presents them. The main body of Weiss's book is devoted to this task. Weiss claims that the core of the so-called Socratic paradoxes is the dictum "no one does wrong willingly" (rather than the more popular maxim "virtue is knowledge"). And she examines six Platonic dialogues where some versions of this dictum can be found:Protagoras,Gorgias,Hippias Minor,Meno,Republic4, andLaws9. Weiss's interpretation of these individual dialogues is always thought-provoking and at times quite illuminating (e.g., her discussion and comparison of Polus and Callicles in theGorgias). Her exposition is quite clear, so the reader knows exactly when he/she disagrees with Weiss's interpretation. And it seems there is a lot to disagree with. Her identifications of the contexts and of Socrates' intentions in those contexts are all very controversial. Since she argues very carefully, it would take too long to discuss in detail all my disagreements with her interpretations of the individual dialogues. Instead, I will focus on some problems of Weiss's general interpretative premises, which I think is more important because the success of her overall project depends ultimately on her interpretative assumptions.

Taking contexts into consideration is, in itself, an impeccable principle. But we need to exercise caution here. There is a chance that the practice may degenerate into what might be called a systematic misunderstanding. Context is supposed to help us determine the correct reading of a certain passage. But one's understanding (or misunderstanding) of certain passages may affect his/her identification of the context. Thus, a good interpretative practice involves taking a balanced approach both to the passages and to the context. But it is unclear if Weiss takes a balanced approach; she seems to put too much emphasis on contexts and reads into the text her own understanding of Socrates' project.

Weiss's identification of contexts is guided by the following two questions.(1) Why is Socrates set up against the particular interlocutors that he converses with in various dialogues?(2) Why does Socrates put forward "ridiculous" views and bad arguments in the dialogues?

The first question is indeed an important one. Posing it is perhaps Weiss's major contribution. But her answer to this question is rather simple: they are sophists, or influenced by sophists, who basically hold that no one is willingly just,[1]and Socrates has a mission to overturn their views. Although this answer may well be true as far as it goes, it does not seem to do full justice to richer and subtler dramatic contexts of various dialogues. If Socrates' objective is simply to refute this sophistic manifesto, it is unclear why Socrates has to deal with different sophists in the various dialogues.Wouldn't one dialogue be sufficient for this purpose?[2]It is undeniable that there were some common grounds among sophists at the time, but it is also true that individual sophists had their own distinctive positions (e.g., Protagoras is not a Callicles, to say the least). Socrates' attack on his opponents seems subtler and more complex than Weiss's wholesale treatment suggests. To be fair, Weiss does note individual differences among different interlocutors. But she relies heavily on character analyses that are based on minute details such as passing remarks and different wordings -- subtle hints that Plato has allegedly dropped for the reader. Again, I'm not denying the dramatic importance of these things. But we need to put them in perspective. Character analysis is only a part of a full-scale dramatic analysis, and the minute details Weiss focuses on are open to other interpretations (e.g. I find Weiss's harsh characterizations of Meno and Hippias, and to a lesser degree of Protagoras, to be a bit strained).

Weiss's answer to the second question consists of two parts:(1) Socrates is not committed to those ridiculous views and bad arguments, and (2) he nonetheless puts them forward because he is on a mission that he cannot afford to lose and those views and arguments are tactically useful for winning. Weiss's main focus throughout the book is on showing how they are tactically useful. On the other hand, surprisingly less effort is devoted to showing that Socrates is indeed not committed to them, though of course this is the presupposition of Weiss's entire project. Weiss seems to be content with showing that it is not impossible to take Socrates as not committed to those views and arguments -- and she is often quite ingenious at this task. Obviously, however, this does not constitute a good reason to think positively that Socrates is indeed not committed to them.

Weiss's reasoning behind being content with this seems to be based on the principle of charity:if what Socrates says seems ridiculous, we should believe that he means something other than what he says, if we can construe him in some other way. Needless to say, there have been a lot of scholars who think that what Socrates says is not ridiculous but demands deep philosophical engagement (even if ultimately it turns out to be false). More importantly, the Socrates that emerges from Weiss's interpretation is not exactly an attractive figure -- something hard to square with the principle of charity. In the following I will indicate some unattractive aspects of Weiss's Socrates. Of course, the fact that Weiss's Socrates is an unattractive figure is not in itself a good argument against her interpretation. But when her unorthodox picture of Socrates is ultimately motivated by the principle of charity, I believe it is a fair criticism.},
               Hegel, Marx, holism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{hayek1952counter,
  author    = {Hayek, Friedrich A.},
  title     = {The Counter-Revolution of Science: Studies on the Abuse of Reason},
  year      = {1952},
  publisher = {The Free Press},
  address   = {Glencoe, IL},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hayek argues that the social sciences underwent a
  "counter-revolution" in the nineteenth century when Saint-Simon, Comte, and
  their followers proposed to reorganize society on "scientific" lines by
  applying the methods of natural science to social phenomena. Hayek calls
  this program "scientism" and argues it is fundamentally misconceived: social
  phenomena are constituted by the meanings, plans, and expectations of
  individual agents, which cannot be captured by the third-person observational
  stance of natural science. The result is a comprehensive defense of
  methodological individualism against collectivist/holist social science and
  against the political programs it generates.

  RELEVANCE: This is the Hayekian complement to Popper's anti-historicism,
  targeting the same positivist-historicist tradition (Comte, Saint-Simon, Marx)
  from the angle of the philosophy of social science rather than pure logic.
  Hayek's critique of "constructivist rationalism" and his contrast between
  spontaneous orders and designed orders provide an alternative vocabulary for
  attacking the speculative tradition's assumption that history's direction is
  rationally transparent and can be engineered.

  POSITION: Hayekian anti-scientism as the second strand of the mid-century
  attack on the speculative tradition; distinct from Popper in emphasis but
  convergent in conclusions.
  },
  keywords = {anti-historicism-philosophy-social-science, Hayek, scientism, abstract = {I am sure most of you have watched one of those marvellous BBC programmes on the life of dinosaurs. It's all happening there before you. The way they walked and ran, their posture, their eating habits, their colour (even of their eyes). It's really impressive. You come to think there is a way to see what happened in the very distant past and that thanks to science (and the BBC, of course) you actually see it. But how is this possible? How can we know (or, at least, have justified beliefs about) what happened in the distant past where no-one was around to keep records and to initiate causal chains of information-transmission to us? Prehistoric events, of course, have left a number of traces; so it must surely be that these are enough (or at least, they are enough under favourable circumstances) to provide premises for reliable inferences that return theories and justified hypotheses about what the past was like. These will be ampliative (hence, defeasible) inferences -- most likely inferences to the best explanation. Isn't science, however, replete with such inferences? There is an epistemic risk involved in getting to know the past, but (you may think) it is different only in degree from the risk involved in getting to know the present (or the future).
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

Things are not so simple, of course. (Why can't things be simple in philosophy?) Perhaps, those who have thought long and hard about history have known this all along. But for those of us who have worked in the philosophy of science, and especially on scientific realism, it might come a surprise to us as that there might be special epistemological problems with the knowledge of the past and that this implies that to be a scientific realist about the past (or about historical sciences, such as geology and paleobiology, and scientific theories in them) is much harder and more risky than to be a realist about the tiny (or about physical sciences, such as physics and chemistry, and scientific theories in them). A thought many of us intellectually grew up with was that if there is a place where it issaferto be a realist than not, it is in sciences other than physics and chemistry, which, after all, deal with unobservedobservables.

I, at least, have been woken from my dogmatic slumbers by Derek Turner's rich and provocative bookMaking Prehistory: Historical Science and the Scientific Realism Debate. There is much to disagree with in this book, but it is an outstanding book on scientific realism -- one that pushes the debate into hitherto uncharted territories.

Turner takes the past to be epistemologically problematic in two important senses -- both of which involve an epistemic asymmetry between the past and the present. First, there is the asymmetry of manipulability, viz., an inability to intervene (to manipulate) the past. Second, there is the asymmetry of the role of background theories, viz., that background theories about the past imply (as background theories about the present and the tiny do not) that a lot of evidence about the past has been irrevocably destroyed and a lot of possible information channels have been dampened. These asymmetries make rampant local underdetermination of theories about prehistory. In a great deal of cases, we are told, scientists face an issue of choice between rival but empirically equivalent and equally epistemically virtuous theories, with nothing in their hands (no means to generate new phenomena, no sources of additional information) to break the tie. There is a third asymmetry, Turner notes, the asymmetry of analogy, viz., that past posits seem to be analogous to current observable entities. This, it might be thought, eases the problem of knowing the past. But Turner argues that it is precisely this asymmetry that explains why scientists have made a number of mistakes about the past. Hence, relying on analogy is not a reliable way to learn about the past. The general conclusion drawn from the three asymmetries is that there are clear senses in which knowing the past is harder than knowing the tiny and hence that scientific realism about historical sciences is in pretty bad shape (and certainly in worse shape than scientific realism about electrons and genes).

This is about half of the book and one might think that the challenge to realism ends there. But (perhaps somewhat surprisingly) Turner takes the past to be ontologically problematic too. There is a certain sense in which we should take the title of the book entirely literally: scientists domakeprehistory. Now, that's not something Turner affirms. But he does not deny it either. He is neutral on this matter. It is a consistent hypothesis that the past is constructed and, for all we know, it might well be constructed. Turner's meta-philosophical stance is anti-metaphysical. Both realism and anti-realism (in all of their guises) impose metaphysical construals on scientific existential claims. They are not content with saying that something exists or is real. Realists add a mind-independence gloss on existence/reality and anti-realists render existence/reality mind-dependent. Turner will have none of this. Does that remind you of something? Right guess! He is a fan of theNatural Historical Attitude, which is (a) an agnostic attitude towards metaphysical questions and (b) a 'gnostic' stance towards historical knowledge -- we do have some knowledge of the past, though its extent should not be exaggerated, as the very idea of an epistemic access to the past is inexorably subject to the aforementioned asymmetries.

This outlines the broader picture. In what follows I will offer a brief critical commentary on the chapters, and I will raise some worries about some of the central planks of Turner's project.

Chapter 1 explores the asymmetries of knowledge and introduces an epistemic distinction between the past and the microphysical (the tiny). Turner claims that we can know more about the tiny than the past. He understands scientific realism as primarily an epistemic position, asserting the knowability of the unobservable, and claims that it is safer to be a scientific realist about the tiny unobservable. When it comes to the past, the defensible position is what he calls "historical hypo-realism". But are past things (e.g., dinosaurs) unobservable? Received wisdom has it that they are not -- at least in the sense that they could be observed by suitably placed observers. Turner disputes received wisdom and claims that dinosaurs and their ilkareunobservable. Part of chapter 1 and most of chapter 3 are about this issue. On top of this, Turner argues that there are two distinct types of unobservable -- the tiny and the past -- and that this typical distinction bears an epistemic weight. Unobservables of type P(ast) are more difficult to be known than of type T(iny).

I have qualms with this, of course. Dinosaurs are unlike electrons in terms of unobservability. The sense in which dinosaurs cannot be seen by the naked (human) eye is different from the sense in which electrons cannot -- different sorts of modalities are involved here. Some laws of nature would have to be violated for either of them to be seen, but (interestingly) seeing dinosaurs (but not electrons) would not require a significant violation of the fundamental laws of nature. The possible world in which dinosaurs are observed is closer to the actual world than the possible world in which electrons are observed. Observability is a matter of degree, but if we care to make a partition among the actually observed, the observable and the unobservable, dinosaurs are closer to the middle than electrons. In any case, are there epistemically distinct types of unobservables? This cannot be an intrinsic difference of course; it will have to do with a principled difference about how an entity can be known by humans. Hence, it will have to do with the methods used to know that something is the case.},
               methodology, holism, individualism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{berlin1969four,
  author    = {Berlin, Isaiah},
  title     = {Four Essays on Liberty},
  year      = {1969},
  publisher = {Oxford University Press},
  address   = {Oxford},
  doi       = {10.2307/446297},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This collection includes "Historical Inevitability" (1954)
  as well as "Two Concepts of Liberty" (1958). "Historical Inevitability"
  argues that any determinist account of history---whether Marxist,
  Hegelian, positivist, or providentialist---is incompatible with the ordinary
  moral vocabulary of praise, blame, and responsibility, which presupposes that
  agents could have acted otherwise. Berlin contends that historians are not
  mere chroniclers but evaluative narrators, and that determinism would
  therefore require abandoning not just historical judgment but ordinary moral
  discourse. "Two Concepts of Liberty" distinguishes negative freedom
  (freedom from interference) from positive freedom (self-mastery), warning
  that positive-liberty theories tend toward political manipulation.

  RELEVANCE: Berlin's "Historical Inevitability" is the third major strand of
  the mid-century anti-historicist critique, attacking developmental-law
  theories on moral-philosophical grounds rather than purely epistemological
  ones. The argument that determinism evacuates moral responsibility is directly
  relevant to critiques of speculative philosophies of history (Hegel, Marx)
  that invoke historical necessity.

  POSITION: Moral-political critique of historical determinism; distinct from
  Popper's epistemological argument and Hayek's methodological one.
  },
  keywords  = {anti-historicism-philosophy-social-science, Berlin, determinism,
               freedom, moral-responsibility, High}
}

